   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">49261</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Biomedical Instrumentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1. Introduction: causes, physiological basis and control techniques (20/100) 2. Analysis and Discussion: physiological basis, advances in technology, cost effectiveness, benefits (60/100) 3. Overall Presentation: readable, logical sequence, free from spelling and grammatical errors, no plagiarism identified (20/100) Students are required to demonstrate a basic inderstanding of disease, medical conditions or physiological conditions. Turnitin report submission: UTSOnline</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Historical Perspective of biomedical instrumentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Literature review</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">25</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Approximately 10 pages with source code if appropriate</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">6</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 6</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Provides basic literature review of a particular biomedical instrumentation Historical Perspective of Biomedical Instrumentation Section 1: Introduction Section 2: Analysis and Discussion Reference List Overall Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1. Introduction: brief description and target application (10/100) 2. Design: design of the system according to the given specifications (40/100) 3. Implementation: build a biomedical system with aesthetic approach (10/100) 4. Results: record results against given specifications (30/100) 5. Conclusion (10/100) Students are required to demonstrate a critical appreciation of various biomedical instruments. Turnitin Report Submission: UTSOnline</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Development of a biomedical system</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">25</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Approximately 15 pages with source code if appropriate</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 11</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Implement a simple biomedical amplifier (ECG or EEG) Section 1: Introduction Section 2: Design Section 3: Implementation Section 4: Results Section 5: Conclusion</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each project team consists of a maximum of four students, and works on a single project for the whole session. It is also possible for students to propose their own projects. Each project may concern the development of a biomedical instrumentation equipment, a biomedical control system, a health-based software system, or a health evaluation system. The assessment of students' work will be based on its quality such as the design ability to meet required specifications and appropriate implementation and testing of solution. There will be two project seminars detailing progress to date. SEMINAR 1: In the first seminar, students in each group are required to present the final specification of their project (approximately 15 minutes). It is expected that they will have finalised the functional block diagram and demonstrated the feasibility of the equipment or software. No hardcopy progress report is required. SEMINAR 2: In the second seminar, students in each group are required to present the methodology, testing and results of their project (approximately 10-15 minutes). Finally, they will be required to give a demonstration of their work. INDIVIDUAL REPORT SUBMISSION: Each student is required to write and submit his/her own project report. A typical project report would have the following format 1. Introduction What are the main aims of the project? Can you find any devices or software which could satisfy these aims? What are their main disadvantages? How can you design a better system? Provide the project specifications. 2. Methods What are the main techniques which are used for the development of this project? Are any novel ideas considered for the project? How can you test your design? Provide detailed methodology? 3. Results What is the performance of the device, software or evaluation system? Have the project specifications met? How thorough is the testing of this system? Has the project been optimised for cost or performance? How does it compare to available commercial devices or systems? 4. Conclusion and future directions Summarise the main points and results of this project. Can this project be improved further? Propose some ideas for future directions. References Appendixes Turnitin Report Submission: UTSOnline</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Major group project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Major Report: approximately 30 pages  Introduction: Aims, literature survey, project specifications (10/100) Methods: modules, methodology, analysis, novel ideas (40/100) Results: Design modules tested, results against specifications, optimisation, quality of hardware/software, photograph (40/100) Conclusion (10/100)</Data></Cell>
