   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">49325</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Computer-aided Mechanical Design</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To apply the FEM to one-dimensional and two-dimensional mechanical design systems for solution of nodal displacements, support reactions and stress. The two quizzes are required to be completed by hand-calculations in class, and the problems in quizzes are in line with the major focuses and examples used in lecture notes, text books and other UTSOnline materials.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The length for an in-class quiz is for up to 45 minutes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">n/a</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class in       Week 3 and Week 5</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 3 and Week 5</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quizzes are designed to help students consolidate key concepts and fundamental knowledge for using finite element method.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To consolidate students' understanding for concepts and fundamentals of mechanics in finite element method, and theories underpin the analysis, such as the isoparametric elements and Gauss integration for two-dimensional problems. The assignments should be basically completed by hand-calculations, and however students can validate their results using software. The problems are also designed based on contents in class, lecture notes, text books and other CANVAS materials.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">60</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Time allowed for an assignment is up to 4 hours on average. Students are required to complete assignment individually and independently.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1-Part A (15%), due 11 August 2020 at 23:59 Assignment 2-Part B (15%), due 25 August 2020 at 23:59 Assignment 3-Part C (15%), due 15 September 2020 at 23:59 Assignment 4-Part D (15%), due 13 October 2020 at 23:59See also Further information.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1-Part A (15%), due 11 August 2020 at 23:59 Assignment 2-Part B (15%), due 25 August 2020 at 23:59 Assignment 3-Part C (15%), due 15 September 2020 at 23:59 Assignment 4-Part D (15%), due 13 October 2020 at 23:59</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Four assignments are designed to help students have a clear understanding of the basic knowledge about simulation and optimization.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate students' understanding for fundamental knowledge about FEM and design optimization gained from class and computer lab, and their capabilities to make use of suitable software to solve mechanical design applications with practical engineering backgrounds. To show students' collaborative working abilities for establishing their own group and devising their own problems in a team environment. A short project description will be provided by the lecturer through UTSOnline to students one week before the project, to guide students for the design of their groups' problems and topics. Every group is required to submit a detailed project report by following a research technical report style, and also email program codes and numerical models to the lectures/tutors. The final marks are group-based.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The overall time for the completion of a group project is up to 30 hours, but it depends.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group Project (30%), due 05 June 2020 at 23:59See also Further information.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">One group project is designed to solidify students' capabilities for collaborative communication and effective team work, and application of knowledge gained in class to resolve real-world design problems using software.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group Project (30%), due 05 June 2020 at 23:59</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A clear understanding of key concepts, main procedures, fundamentals of mechanics including theory of elasticity, and a systematic integration of these basic knowledge into the finite element method and design optimization technology. Knowing the formulation of design problem and the numerical model of optimization, as well as the overall process for performing a general design optimization for mechanical structural problems. The problems in the final exam will be mainly focused on one and two-dimensional problems to use finite element analysis, which will align with the types of design problems in lectures, quizzes and assignments. This is an open book exam.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
