   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41077</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Data Driven and Intelligent Robotics</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The design project includes a comprehensive design and plan of a prototype, which will include the following deliverables:  An empathy map developed from interviews with peers; How-might-we statements and framing of the expected robotic application; Storyboard of the expected robotic application; Personality and interaction behaviour scripts; Identification of appropriate and feasible technologies for implementing the application.  Students will work as a group and will be allocated a group mark. Teaching staff may vary individual marks of students on the basis of their individual contribution who are found to have not contributed meaningfully to the deliverables.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design a data-driven and intelligent robotic application.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design/drawing/plan/sketch</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">No required word limit, however it is suggested that submissions are approximately five pages.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">17/08/2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Saturday 17 August 2019              Due by electronic submission on 17/08/2019 (Saturday of Week 4)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due by electronic submission on 17/08/2019 (Saturday of Week 4)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate an understanding of a design process and to prepare a viable plan for a prototype to be implemented as part of Assessment 2 and 3.  Students will apply Human-robot interaction best practices in the design of the system. Students will be able to synthesise and prioritise choices, especially in terms of their technical and time feasibility.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The development of the prototype will include the following sub-tasks:   The strategic selection of a subset of designed features to implement in the prototype;  Working source code that implements the selected features on a real robot platform;  Demonstration of professional software development practices and methodologies;  Integration of a data-driven intelligent algorithm from data analytics or artificial intelligence, within the implemented robotic application.   Students will work as a group and will be allocated individual marks on the basis of their individual contributions as assessed by git commit logs and through a peer assessment process.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Implement a prototype data-driven and intelligent robot application.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class, Week 12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class, Week 12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate an ability to deliver data-driven intelligent robotic applications.  Students will translate their design specifications into functioning robotic behaviours. The student will acquire leadership and teamwork skills by working in a team to deliver professional code that makes use of data analytics, artificial intelligence and insights into data-driven intelligent robots.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The assignment will consist of a written report and seminar that includes the following sub-tasks:   An analysis of tests conducted using the prototype, demonstrating insights into the limitations of the design and the integrated machine learning algorithms;  An analysis of the experimental methodology used throughout the development and testing of the prototype;  A formulation of new hypotheses arising from tests of the prototype;  A technical summary of the contributions including the design, implementation and tests of the application in a technical report that draws connections to assigned readings and in-class discussions;  A clear and engaging presentation to an audience of peers and academic researchers.   Students will work as a group but will be allocated individual marks based on their individual contributions as assessed by tracked artefacts (such as edit logs) and through a peer assessment process.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report and seminar on data-driven and intelligent robotics</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">No more than 8 pages report and 5 minute presentation + 10 minutes questions.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will choose from a selection of presentation slots that will be made available during the assessment weeks. The written report must be uploaded to UTS Online before the presentation slot begins.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate a comprehensive understanding of data-driven and intelligent robotics including an ability to make insightful connections between theory, practice and experimental results.  Students will demonstrate an ability to think as researchers by testing the limitations of the integrated machine learning algorithm and by formulating clear hypotheses related to human-robot interaction research. The student will acquire the ability to provide sound methodologies to evaluate the designed and implemented robotic application by considering legislative, ethical and social implications. The student will be able to clearly communicate the choices and outcomes to an audience.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will choose from a selection of presentation slots that will be made available during the assessment weeks. The written report must be uploaded to UTS Online before the presentation slot begins.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
