   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48240</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design and Innovation Fundamentals</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each group will have to design a product or service through the stages of problem definition, concept generation, requirements analysis, system design, detailed design, the production of an initial prototype (alpha prototype) and gaining feedback on the prototype. The details of the project and the design constraints can be found in the detailed project description on UTSOnline. The project work will be managed within tutorial session and workshops will provide guidance and activities that will be used in project submissions. Students may need to meet with team members and contribute to the project work outside of workshop and tutorial times in order to successfully complete the project. While project tasks may be distributed between group members, it is compulsory that each group member participates in the entire design process. Students may be asked questions on any aspect of the design project as part of their oral presentation. How well a student answers these questions will form part of their assessment. The project is made up of the following deliverables which guide students through the design process and support team work: 1. DISCOVERY: Project Context Report - 5% An individual report is handed in by each student before project group work begins. The report must investigate the context of the project, identifying three areas where the potential exists for an innovative design to solve an existing problem. This report requires students to show understanding of the context of the project and prepares each student to join their group with a sound grounding in the project scenario. 2. SCOPING: Group Interim Report - 10% The interim design report identifies the scope of the problem the group aims to solve within the project context. The report will detail how the group selected a specific problem to solve. It will also include a prioritised needs analysis, description of constraints, a discussion of the potential business opportunity and a range of potential solutions (with the current leading candidate identified) for the problem along with details on how those ideas were generated. One report is handed in by each group. Individual marks moderated by SPARK1. 3. PITCHING: Solution and Prototype Pitches - 5% Through the project execution, each group will be required to present their ideas and progress to other groups. This assessment is focused on developing presentation skills and collaboration. Each group is required to complete BOTH the solution and prototype pitches and every student must present at least once, however not every student needs to talk at each presentation. At each presentation, at least 2 students should present. If any student presents more than once, their higher mark will count. At each presentation, those group members not presenting are required to take not of feedback to incorporate into their later designs. SOLUTION PITCH. Preliminary Solution Presentation This is a group presentation within your tutorial session where each group will present their problem, their requirements and the solution they have selected along with justification for the choice and an explanation of how the solution meets the requirements, constraints and is innovative. As part of this presentation, other groups are required to ask clarifying questions and give feedback and suggestions for the presenting group. PROTOTYPE PITCH. Prototype Presentation This is a group presentation within your tutorial session where each group will present their initial prototype, explaining its characteristics and how they will go about assessing its suitability to meet requirements. This should include consideration of design for manufacture and design for assembly along with other design considerations (DFx). As part of this presentation, other groups are required to ask clarifying questions and give feedback and suggestions for the presenting group. 4. REPORTING: Final Design Report and presentation - 40% The final design assessment is made up of two parts: a design report and presentation and a prototype. Each deliverable is handed in once by each group. Individual marks moderated by SPARK 2. WRITTEN REPORT: Design Brief - 20% A detailed report presenting the final design including information on, and justification for, the design decisions taken through the process. The report should be aimed at the target audience and draw conclusions on the suitability of the design and future steps to be taken. ORAL PRESENTATION: Group oral presentation - 20% A presentation made by the whole group on the final design highlighting the design process and tools used in developing the design and making decisions through the process. 5. REVIEW: Individual Project Collaboration Statement and Reflection - 20% Individuals will be assessed on how well they function and collaborate within their team and with other groups. This report will document how the student has applied design and innovation tools and processes within their project. Students will need to complete the following tasks and write a Statement of Contribution giving evidence of their activities. Please refer to UTSOnline for suggestion of evidence of activities.  Team contract Constructive contribution to self and peer assessment in Spark Participation in team meetings and collaborative exercises Constructive use of feedback from peers in design process  TOTAL: 80 marks</Data></Cell>
