   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">95566</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Digital Experience Design</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Consists of research findings from two main sources: (1) published research articles; and (2) commercial offerings of related products. In particular, students will need to search, read, evaluate and produce findings from these sources any related existing efforts to understand or to design for a type of meaningful experience that arises from people’s interactions with music. This must be a type of experience that is valued or is desirable to people. This document is important because it will provide a review of previous research findings and commercial efforts; giving the reader a broad understanding of what has been already done. In other words, what have others done to understand this particular experience or technologies that have been already designed to support this particular experience? This review is carried out before we conduct user research.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Music listening Design Research report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Under 2000 words, not including references</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 5              Due by 6.00pm on the day of your allocated class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due by 6.00pm on the day of your allocated class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task will be assessed according to the degree to which you are able to:  clearly describe, explain and justify the value of a type of experience that is appropriate and meaningful to the users identified; present a coherent and detailed review and critique of related efforts through background and desk research, and identify relevant research questions to pursue during user research; produce a coherent report that contains arguments and ideas that are thorough and detailed; use Harvard Referencing correctly in a research report; integrate, include and apply the theories of Experience-Centered Design in the report.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task is intended to be the research that informs your Design Project. Therefore, this task is linked to Assessment Task 2.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will work in a group of 3-4 to reimagine/redesign a digital technology system/app or service that can better support desirable and appropriate experiences when interacting with music. This task enables students to apply understandings from Assessment Task 1 to a project. The task requires students to participate in offline and online activities in which they will:  conduct relevant user and technology/product research, and discuss your findings in a design brief with consideration to the target audience’s needs and desired experiences (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) pitch your project idea (presented in class) and form a group to build upon one project idea develop 3 user personas (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) present 3 design ideas (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) develop 3 user experience stories and task breakdowns based on your user research and personas (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) develop a rough/hand-drawn/low fidelity storyboard or interface narrative based on one of your user experience stories and tasks (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) produce a flowchart of use-actions and equivalent interface or technology interactions based on your storyboard (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) produce a system (technical) blueprint for the proposed design of your product/system/service, showing the components and the structure and hierarchy of interfaces (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) create a prototype for the interaction and information design solution you have offered showing actual interactivity of movement for a key task (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas) produce a brief user experience evaluation report based on your evaluation findings and how it informed the iterations of your prototype hold a group presentation in class about the project write a 2000-word rationale describing the decision-making processes informing why particular aspects were designed in such a way, with reference to the design considerations as well as referring to the theories of Experience-Centred Design (to be posted on a designated Discussion board on Canvas)  All the constituent parts of the assessment are based on tutorial activities and online activities. So, attendance and participation are imperative. All elements of the assessment task must have been submitted in a timely manner. While this assignment has various elements, it will only be assessed as a whole when it is submitted in its entirety by the due date.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Music Experience Design project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design/drawing/plan/sketch</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Rationale 2000 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable              Deliverables due Wks 7, 8, 11 and 12. Check the detailed assignment document in Canvas.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Deliverables due Wks 7, 8, 11 and 12. Check the detailed assignment document in Canvas.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task will be assessed according to:   how comprehensively you have applied and reflected on your research findings in your overall design  how effectively you have justified your design decisions with appropriate reference to your own research and other theories of experience, information and interaction design  evidence of learning and progression at each stage of the project, including what inspired you to revise your ideas.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To provide provide students with opportunities to practice a humanistic Experience-Centred Design methodology to to reimagine/redesign a digital technology system/app or service that can better support desirable and appropriate experiences</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection is a very important part of learning. One of the aims of the weekly online and class discussions is to support you to become aware of your own thinking processes, and being able to make those transparent to others. This assignment is an assessment of your participation, contribution and your developing ability to reflect on the subject matter taught in this subject and your own thinking and views about Experience-Centered Design over the course of the semester. You should participate in the online activities to:  contribute your reflections to various set topics related to the subject collaborate and share information with other students seek answers to anything you feel you do not fully understand about the subject, topics, or assessment tasks continue debating issues raised in class avoid feeling lost or isolated when working by yourself on your assessment tasks.  To guide our assessment of your participation and contributions, you will need a submit a Participation and Contribution document by 23:59 of Friday 5 June, 2020. You must attach the UTS assignment cover sheet at the front of the document. This document will contain two main sections: Section 1: A written reflection on your learning about different aspects of the subject, the progress of your skills in designing for Experience-Centered Design and anything else reflective that demonstrate your development, growth, learning in the subject over the course of the semester. This written reflection should be under 500 words. It should be written in the first-person. Section 2: Samples of evidence of participation/contributions  This contains screenshots from at least 5 (five) discussion forums that best illustrate the quality of your engagements during the teaching session. Each screenshot should be accompanied by a short statement about why you chose this example.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Class and online participation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2000 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">05/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Friday 5 June 2020              While you should be participating throughout the semester, you will need to upload your participation document by 11.59pm Friday 5 June 2020.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The contributions you make should be carefully considered and written, and you will be assessed over the semester according to the extent to which you:  engage with other people’s contributions and provide constructive feedback  refer to and reflect on readings from the textbook and other reference materials incorporate your personal and professional experience  show evidence of and offer ideas from wider reading and research, which may include suggesting additional resources relevant to the topic appropriately acknowledge the sources you used in Harvard style.  The online discussions are open-ended, but this does not mean it is acceptable to simply do a mass posting at the end of semester. See Assignment 3 description on Canvas for instructions and further details.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">While you should be participating throughout the semester, you will need to upload your participation document by 11.59pm Friday 5 June 2020.</Data></Cell>
