   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">95564</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Digital Media Technologies</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The tasks consist of 3 components: 1. REFLECTIVE BLOGGING In this class, we use blogs to write about our work, what happens, and what you know about it. This is a mandatory part of class participation. “Blogging isn’t really about writing at all; that’s just the end point of the process, the outcome that occurs more or less naturally if everything else has been done right. Blogging is about, first, reading. But more important, it is about reading what is of interest to you: your culture, your community, your ideas. And it is about engaging with the content and with the authors of what you have read—reflecting, criticizing, questioning, reacting.” Stephen Downes: EDUCAUSE Review, vol. 39, no. 5 (September/October 2004): 14–26. This activity requires you to:  Find, read and critique online articles/resources relating to your project, technologies or digital media in general. Post to the blog your critique and thoughts relating to the items you have read, including a link back to the online resource. Comment on the critiques posted by your peers.  2. DRAFTS OF WORK-IN-PROGRESS You are required to show how your work evolves over time by submitting drafts of your learning proposal, Technology Analysis and System Plan. You are also expected to provide peer feedback on other students' drafts. As a minimum, you must post drafts of your Learning Proposal, Technology Analysis and System Plan, and comment on the others' drafts. 3. ATTENDANCE AND CLASS PARTICIPATION Students are strongly encouraged to attend 80% or more of classes and to participate in class and online discussion each week.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Participation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Throughout the semester</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Throughout the semester</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Please note: Weighting of Assessment Criteria is approximate. Please see your subject guide for mark allocation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task seeks to develop skills in critical reflection and analysis, information literacy as well as academic research and writing. It therefore meets all objectives of the subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Your learning proposal is linked to all the other assessment tasks for the subject. Since participants in this subject are likely to have different learning needs according to their prior experience, discipline base and involvement in the multimedia industry, this task recognises that each of you will have different expectations of what you want to learn. Consequently, this assessment is produced in four steps:  You hand in a draft learning proposal Your draft will be reviewed and you will be given written feedback You hand in a final learning proposal. You present a short ‘project pitch’ in class  This proposal also encourages you to plan your learning tasks for the semester. Therefore, it should include:  a personal SWOT analysis that identifies and justifies your learning needs learning objectives and strategies and resources. Strategies should be specific, quantifiable, conditional and relate to the fulfilment of your final project. a detailed description of your proposed project and the form it will take.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Learning Proposal</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">500 Words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">04/04/2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">03:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">3.00pm Tuesday 4 April 2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Please note: Weighting of Assessment Criteria is approximate. Please see your subject guide for mark allocation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">After assessing your learning needs, this exercise allows you to clarify your individual aims, duties, learning needs, objectives and strategies.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This analysis should demonstrate your understanding and assessment of the range of possible technology choices for your proposed project. The report should discuss and analyse each of the major technologies you have chosen: In terms of “macro” considerations, for example:  Is the technology proprietary or open source? Is it a standard or based on a standard of some kind? How good is the community or commercial support for the technology? Does it support the development of accessible systems? Does it support multi-language use and other kinds of internationalisation? How does it fit into the architecture of the web? Who uses it and what for? What is the market for the technology like?  In terms of “micro” considerations, for example:  How does it fit with the other technologies you have chosen? What kind of technology is it? Why is it suitable for the task you are using it for? What qualities of the technology itself make it suitable for the task? Is it a good match for your skills and needs? What place does it occupy in the architecture of your proposed system?  These points are just examples to show what the scale of analysis could be. You don’t have to address all of these and you can address questions at the appropriate scale that aren’t on this list.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Technology Analysis</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1500 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">16/05/2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">03:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">3.00pm Tuesday 16 May 2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Please note: Weighting of Assessment Criteria is approximate. Please see your subject guide for mark allocation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This exercise is an opportunity to read more broadly about the technology choices in your proposed project, develop an argument justifying those choices and document your reasoning.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task should demonstrate how you plan to integrate your understanding of the technologies you have chosen with your grasp of web application architectures learned in class, and apply them to your proposed project. It is also intended to foster an ability to plan ahead and scope a project. The report should provide a plan for the system you intend to build in your final research project. It could include, for example:  a written 'sketch' or ‘persona’ of the kinds of people you envision using your system a summary of the kinds of activities that people could use your system for (or a scenario of use) a visual sketch of the major screens of the application a site map describing how the pages of the system link to each other an architecture diagram of how the component technologies and parts of your application fit together.  These should serve as examples to indicate the kind of documentation that might make up a system plan. Your plan need not include all of these and may include sections not mentioned in this list.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">System Plan</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design/drawing/plan/sketch</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1000 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">23/05/2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">03:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">3.00pm Tuesday 23 May 2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Please note: Weighting of Assessment Criteria is approximate. Please see your subject guide for mark allocation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task is intended to demonstrate your understanding of the technology choices you have made for your proposed project and how you plan to apply them.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Projects MUST NOT be based on projects you have previously done (in another course, or at work for example) unless you have written permission from the lecturer. Projects may be built using frameworks, code libraries, platforms and templates; however, every source must be fully cited and explained. Only standards-based web technologies are appropriate for this subject. Proprietary formats may only be used if you can demonstrate to your lecturer that the desired effect cannot be achieved with a standards-based approach. Your site will be checked with the W3C validator. The system you have built must be available on a publicly accessible web server in order to be assessed. Your report should include the URL of the site. Systems or web pages handed in on disks or other media will not be assessed and you will fail this assessment task. This reflection can include revised text from the other assessment tasks compiled into a single valid XHTML or HTML5 web page. It should clearly show where you have made new additions to the text: that is, it should be primarily reflective and explain what you did and did not do, the reasons why, and what you would have liked to do differently. The reflection must include evidence that your application or web site conforms to W3C standards for both XHTML and CSS.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final project: website and reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1000 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20/06/2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">03:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">3.00pm Tuesday 20 June 2017</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The task represents the realisation of your Learning Proposal, demonstration of your newly acquired skills and knowledge of your chosen technology(s) of interest.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
