   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48660</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Dynamics and Control</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is the first part of Project 1 which requires students to solve a practical problem from modelling, analysis and experimental verfification. Details of the tasks and requirements are stated in the Project-Part 1 description. Below is a list of the tasks: (1) Complete the MATLAB On-Ramp online course. (2) Read the provided material for detailed information about the dynamic system being modelled; (3) Collect data using the methods specified in the project description; (4) Define the input and output (including signal flow and units) of the components; (5) Develop the transfer functions of these components.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project 1 - Part 1: Modelling of a practical dynamic system</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">13/04/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 13 April 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project 2 requires students to solve a 2 degree-of-freedom dynamic problem. This project consists of three parts: Part 1: Develop a dynamic model of the 2DOF dynamic system; Part 2: Analyse the dynamic response with base motion; Part 3: Simulation and/or experimental testing using a 2DOF shaker table rig; This project provides the opportunity for students to apply the theory to solve a 2DOF dynamic system. Details of the tasks and requirements are stated in the Project description.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project 2: Modelling of a 2 DOF system (Part 1), vibration analysis (Part 2) and experimental verification (Part 3)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">27/04/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 27 April 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The lab sessions provide the opportunity for students to interact and characterise a range of real world dynamic systems, and reflect on the difference between theoretical and practical systems. Through these sessions, students will gain a better understanding of the behaviour of dynamic systems that are typically encountered in the practice of engineering; and gain an understanding of how such systems can be controlled, or have their dynamics altered, so as to achieve desired outcomes Each lab session will be held in the Dynamics and Mechanics of solids Laboratory, where experiments and demonstrations will be supervised by a member of the teaching staff. Experiments 1 &amp; 2 are performed on the IDOF Free and Forced Vibration Apparatus (cussons rig), and demonstrations illustrate dynamic absorbers and vibration control. Students are required to (1) Conduct the experiments in a group; (2) Record data from the experiments and demonstrations; (3) Individually write a lab report comprised of a report-style description and analysis of experiments 1 and 2.  The report will be marked as an individual project.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11/05/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 11 May 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment covers parts two and three of Project 1, which requires students to solve a practical problem from modelling, analysis and experimental verfification. This project provides the opportunity for students to apply the theory to solve a real world practical dynamics and control problem. Details of the tasks and requirements are stated in the description. Below is a list of the tasks: (1) Based on a model of the system, you are required to design P and PD controllers to control the system; (2) Verify the step response properties useing MATLAB and Simulink; (3) Validate experimentally the controllers you designed; (4) Write a report which includes controller design, simulated results, discussions on the difference between the simulated and experimental results, and reflection on the experiments.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project 1 - Parts 2 and 3: Design of controllers for a system (Part 2); and experimental verification (Part 3)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">13</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
