   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41023</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Early-stage Prototyping</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A Design Portfolio depicts an individual’s curated learning journey or roadmap of their individual and team experience of leading for innovation during early-stage prototyping. Each individual in a team will develop a unique perspective on their journey and will submit an individual portfolio. The Design Portfolio is an ePortfolio in the format of student’s own choice. Students can use the Learning Management System (LMS) ePortfolio tool or develop their own using other platforms (e.g. Weebly, WordPress). The Design Portfolio should begin with an introduction to themselves, their team, and their team project(s) and the intended target audience for the prototype. The Design Portfolio should include a summary of the top strengths and blind spots listed in the student’s Fingerprint for Success assessment, the student’s reflection on their F4S assessment and the student’s learning contract. The ePortfolio should be written from the student’s individual perspective and be original work describing making process throughout the prototyping process as seen through the inclusion of artefacts and reflections. The ePortfolio is a depiction of experience and reflection and must include each of the following:  Students will collect and present artefacts (see list online for possible inclusions) and include examples of their project design, development, implementation steps and feedback received on their ePortfolio during each sprint (each week). Evidence of Team Pitch and reflection. A summary that synthesises the individual’s development and achievement of the subject learning outcomes and their personal learning goals throughout the subject journey.  Reflective statements in the ePortfolio should clearly articulate and demonstrate engagement with key learning activities (including data collected and/or the incorporation of expert, facilitator, and peer feedback, as well as, information that emerges from systematic group progress review each week during the sprints).  The reflective statements must demonstrate what the student learned about themselves and their unique perspectives on the group dynamics related to leading innovation in the future of work. These reflective statements should be aligned or linked to the subject learning objectives and the unique learning objectives selected in the learning contract.  The relevant artefacts in the ePortfolio should be chosen by the student to form a coherent and cumulative substantiation of claims of achievement of each learning objective in the subject and the learning contract. Reflection on the Design Thinking Process is a final reflective statement written from the individual students’ perspective and should include a synthesized student’s learning journey. Students demonstrate their achievement using a collection of artefacts together with reflections that meet the Performance Standard Criteria to Pass, below.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">100</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">First iteration of the Design Portfolio is submitted in Week 1 and presented in class the next day.  The team presentation on the final version of the prototyping is submitted electronically by 11:59 pm in the final week of class and presented in class on the following day.  Final version of the Design Portfolio including the student’s Reflection on the Design Thinking Process is submitted in the final week of class and presented in class the next day.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">First iteration of the Design Portfolio is submitted in Week 1 and presented in class the next day.  The team presentation on the final version of the prototyping is submitted electronically by 11:59 pm in the final week of class and presented in class on the following day.  Final version of the Design Portfolio including the student’s Reflection on the Design Thinking Process is submitted in the final week of class and presented in class the next day.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
