   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48570</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Embedded Systems Studio B</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">At the start of the teaching session, students create an individual learning contract (ILC) and then have it agreed to by their academic mentor(s). The ILC should describe a series of activities that will enable the student to gain knowledge and skills in the area of embedded systems, particularly to enhance the student’s contribution to the team and the product to be developed. Students generally complete this individual learning independently and outside of class time, and check in regularly with their academic mentor to update progress, receive feedback and identify any challenges encountered. Assessment of the ILC will take place in three phases: ILC setup and agreement at the start of session ILC maintenance and monitoring during the session ILC finalisation at the end of session Students document their individual learning in a portfolio. At the end of session, each student will complete a viva voce with their academic mentor to explain their individual learning.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual learning contract</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ILC setup/agreement (5%) due 8:00PM on 25/03/2020; ILC maintenance/monitoring (10%) due 8:00PM on 29/04/2020; ILC finalisation (15%) due 8:00PM on 03/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ILC setup/agreement (5%) due 8:00PM on 25/03/2020; ILC maintenance/monitoring (10%) due 8:00PM on 29/04/2020; ILC finalisation (15%) due 8:00PM on 03/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To provide an opportunity for guided self-learning of topics related to embedded systems.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students work in teams to design and implement an embedded systems product. Each student individually must prepare a portfolio documenting their contributions, design decisions and insights relating to the product throughout the teaching session. Weekly updates are expected, with each update building on and extending the previous work in the portfolio. By the final submission at the end of session, the portfolio should be a complete record of the work undertaken. Students must submit their portfolio for assessment and feedback a minimum of 5 times during the session, but may submit up to a maximum of 10 times. The weighting of each submission varies according to the number of submissions.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Embedded systems project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly on Wednesdays at 8:00PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly on Wednesdays at 8:00PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate the ability to design, implement, test and evaluate a product based around an embedded system.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Teams of students prepare and present a final report describing the product that was implemented. The report should describe design choices, technology choices, and the implementation and testing strategy. A separate section should provide an evaluation of the product, to identify benefits and limitations of the approach chosen, what you learned along the way and what you might do differently in a future embedded systems design. As a team, students also need to deliver a short presentation of their implemented product to their peers and teaching staff. The presentation is to include technical aspects of the product design as well as a reflection on how collaboration as a team impacted on the quality of the product. All team members should contribute to the presentation. The audience for both of these deliverables is industry professional engineers.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report: 5000 words Presentation: 10 minutes presentation, followed by a Q&amp;A session</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate professional written and oral communication skills in documenting and describing an embedded systems product.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
