   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">42015</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Engineering Practice in an Australian Context</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1a) Students need to determine a platform in which they intend to design their Employability Portfolio. It is expected that their portfolio will have most of following:  a ‘landing page’ which establishes the portfolio writer’s professional identity by including a photograph of the portfolio writer in a professional or study situation, and an ‘about me’ section that explains the writers engineering specialization and why they have chosen this specialisation. a resume (students are expected to seek the assistance of UTS Careers to ensure they have a resume that will maximize their chances of getting an engineering internship or job). a selection of engineering internship or job advertisements and an analysis of what these advertisements communicate about what employers are looking for. It is expected that students will indicate how they will communicate their own employability in relation to these advertisements, e.g in the form of job application cover letters, or capability statements. It is expected that students will write brief recounts of what they learned i) in the preparation activities for each class, ii) what they learned in the class, and iii) what activity they engaged in after the class. At least one ‘photovoice’ text. This involves students taking a photo either while engaged in a study activity at UTS, or if they have a job, in their workplace. The photo for this activity should be of something that shows a part of their study or work life of which other people may not be aware. Students should then write approximately 400-500 words about the photo explaining why they have taken the photo and what it means to the student. One critical incident journal entry. This involves students thinking of a time when they had an experience that they learned a lot from.  Students need to make the URL for their portfolio available to their Peer Review Group. Then they need to review their peers’ portfolios and be prepared to give feedback to their peers on their portfolios in tutorial 4.  1b) Students will improve the work already contained in the portfolios based on the feedback they have received from peers. Additionally students will utilise the photovoice and critical incident methodologies to produce pieces of writing on some of the following topics:  What is engineering practice? Becoming a professional communicator How to observe and learn in the workplace How to talk about ethical lapses What attitudes and capabilities are necessary to be an active participant in a workplace  Finally, students will need to prepare a statement about how they have addressed the Subject Intended learning Outcomes and what evidence they have to support this statement.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Curated Employability Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">100</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Task 1a) due two days prior to tutorial 4; Task 1b) due two days prior to tutorial 6.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Task 1a) due two days prior to tutorial 4; Task 1b) due two days prior to tutorial 6.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The Curated Employability Portfolio communicates to a reader how the student has fulfilled the subject learning objectives (SLOs). Students are expected to curate the artefacts of their inquiry into how to obtain a job, the capabilities they need in order to contribute to a workplace, and how to learn in a workplace. It is anticipated that their portfolios will include a resume, and sample job advertisements with analysis of what the advertisements communicate about what employers are looking for. It is also anticipated that students will explore methodologies, such as photovoice, critical incident journaling, in order to conduct inquiry into workplace learning, and to present the findings of their inquiry.</Data></Cell>
