   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41055</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Engineering Workplace Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task has 3 parts. Firstly, you will need to:  Draft an ‘about me’ page for your portfolio and attach the latest version of your resume. Draft two pieces of reflective writing on the topics below using the information about reflective writing provided in the week 1 lecture, and the further explanation provided in the Learning Guide (pp.10-13) (approx. 700 words each) Apply principles of sustainability to create viable systems (A4) Professional Practice within intercultural and global contexts (F)  Submit your Learning Plan to your peer review group by the due date 1b) Go to your Peer Review Group in UTSOnline and review your peers’ work to give feedback in the first tutorial. 1c) Write a paragraph summarising the feedback you have received from your peers in tutorial 1 and include any decisions you have made about how you intend to change your work. Note: Marks for this assessment task will only be awarded to students who have uploaded their own work, reviewed peers’ work, provided verbal feedback in their timetabled tutorial, and lodged a summary of the feedback they have received by the due dates designated in the Program section of this subject outline. As outlined in the Late Penalty clause c) section of this Subject Outline, students who fail to meet all requirements of the assessment including attendance at the tutorial will receive zero for Assessment Task 1. These are essential components of Course Intended Learning Outcomes E.2 &amp; F.1.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Review of peers’ draft reflections, updated resume and landing page</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Approximately 2,500 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment task 1a – due 11:59 pm Sunday, 2 August; Assessment task 1b – due 5:00 pm Friday, 7 August; Assessment task 1c – due 11:59 pm Sunday, 16 August</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment task 1a – due 11:59 pm Sunday, 2 August; Assessment task 1b – due 5:00 pm Friday, 7 August; Assessment task 1c – due 11:59 pm Sunday, 16 August</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The review of peers’ draft reflections, resume and about me section is designed to provide students with the opportunity to develop their professional practice portfolios, and to give and receive feedback from peers on their work.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task has four parts: 2a) Students are required to continue reviewing an online professional practice portfolio that details some of their professional experiences. Their portfolio should:  commence with a ‘landing page’ that includes an ‘about me’ section that is a brief autobiographical narrative of their journey to becoming an engineer and the key influences on their choice of specialisation (major)include their updated resume/curriculum vitae. include written reflection on the 2 topics designated in task 1 above (approximately 700 words on each topic). Each written reflection should refer to some theoretical literature (see subject resources in Canvas) using the APA referencing system. Include a cover letter addressing the selection criterion on page 15 of the ‘Learning Guide.’  Students are required to make the URL for their draft portfolios available to their Peer Review Group by the due date. 2b) Go to your Peer Review Group in UTS online to obtain the URLs of your peers’ portfolios. Students review their peers’ draft portfolios and make comments on Canvas. Students are required to provide verbal constructive critical feedback to their peer group in the tutorial. Following the tutorial, students should utilise the review feedback they have received to improve their portfolios for final submission. 2c) A paragraph summarising feedback received from their peers must be submitted after the tutorial to receive marks for this task. Note: marks for this assessment task will only be awarded to students who have uploaded their own work, reviewed peers’ work, provided verbal feedback in their timetabled tutorial, and submitted a summary of the feedback they have received from peers by the due dates designated in the Program section of this subject outline. As outlined in the Late Penalty clause c) section of this Subject Outline, students who fail to meet all requirements of the assessment including attendance at the tutorial will receive zero for Assessment Task 2. These are essential components of Course Intended Learning Outcomes E.2 &amp; F.1.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Critical review of peers' professional practice portfolios</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2a) draft portfolio 4,000 words (before tutorial); and 2c) paragraph summarising feedback from peers (after tutorial)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment task 2a – due 11.59pm Sunday, 23 August;  Assessment task 2b - due 5:00pm Friday, 28 August;  Assessment task 2c - due 11.59pm Sunday 6 September.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment task 2a – due 11.59pm Sunday, 23 August;  Assessment task 2b - due 5:00pm Friday, 28 August;  Assessment task 2c - due 11.59pm Sunday 6 September.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The critical review of peers’ draft professional practice portfolios gives students the opportunity to give and receive feedback as a basis for reflection on how to improve their portfolios for final submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Utilising feedback provided by peers in the week 6 tutorial, students are required to finalise a curated professional practice portfolio demonstrating ability to:  reflect on professional experience, conduct critical self-review and plan for professional development; articulate in writing the development of their professional identity to date and use these articulations to inform a response to selection criteria in a job advertisement.  Students’ finalised portfolios should demonstrate reader awareness by presenting information in a well organised and easy to navigate format with a professional level of language communication proficiency evidencing effective editing skills, and utilising APA referencing conventions where needed.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Curated professional practice portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">4,000 words</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">21/09/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">09:00 AM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">9.00am Monday 21 September 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An online professional practice portfolio that provides a reader with a narrative overview of the portfolio writer in terms of academic and professional motivations and experience, and presents a high quality, professional Resume/ Curriculum Vitae. The portfolio presents two pieces of reflective writing. Each piece of reflective writing demonstrates the student’s ability to:   recount/describe experience; and   react to and evaluate that experience including identifying feelings, and/or reflecting upon personal beliefs, and/or recognizing and analyzing difficulties; and   relate the experience to academic, professional, or broader literature related to the topic, referencing this literature in accordance with APA conventions, evidencing literate engagement with the topics; and   explain what lessons have been learned (retrospective outcomes), and what the student’s future intentions are (prospective outcomes) in relation to each topic   A cover letter responding to selection criteria in a job advertisement. The letter should be clearly ordered and formatted. It should address each criteria separately to demonstrate how the writer’s experiences qualify him/her for the position. Language use communicates meaning clearly with a professional/formal degree of accuracy indicative of careful editing. The portfolio is clearly organised and easy for a reader to navigate.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The curated professional practice portfolio is designed to provide students with the opportunity to capture in writing a critical self-review and reflection of critical incidents in their work experience in relation to the Engineers Australia Stage 1 Professional and Personal Attributes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will be in online seminar groups of 3 students. Students will be given approximately 20 minutes each for their interview. Each interview will consist of the following:  Students will be interviewed by a tutor/assessor who will ask questions about their Engineering field of practice/major and motivations for choosing this major.  The assessor will ask the student questions to explore critical incidents and/or other dimensions of the students’ academic and professional experiences.   An industry representative (if such representatives are available) or fellow students may be invited to share their professional experience in relation the topics the student has addressed, or ask the student questions.   The assessor will ask the student to reflect on what the Industry Representative/peers have shared and to draw future developmental implications.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Interview</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20-30 minutes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Seminar  week 10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Seminar  week 10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The interview is designed to assess students’ ability to establish a professional identity in verbal communication and to respond to questions about their professional experience.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Make a 10-minute presentation - based on your work experience activities - that critically reviews your experience, highlights your contributions to your workplace, profiles your development as a future engineer and reflects succinctly on issues influencing professional practice. You should be prepared to answer questions from the audience, comprising other EWR students presenting in the same session, as well as EPP1 students and the session facilitator. There may be other staff members present as well as industry representatives. Details are contained in the learning guide</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The seminar presentation slides are due online immediately prior to your seminar session</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The seminar presentation slides are due online immediately prior to your seminar session</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">refer to the learning guide</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">to develop your oral presentation skills</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
