   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41188</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Enterprise Process Management</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this individual assignment students are required to: (i) design a high-level process architecture (also known as process landscape) for the given real-life scenario; (ii) use the architecture to draw a process portfolio; and (iii) prioritize the process and nominate the best candidate for process improvement. Each student is expected to produce a written report describing their proposed solution. A group feedback will be provided in class. Individual feedback will be provided online.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Process architecture assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Maximum 6 pages</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15/04/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">05:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5.00pm Wednesday 15 April 2020              (Week 6)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">(Week 6)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design of a high-level conceptual process architecture (process landscape).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task builds upon learning in Task 1, as students continue to explore a real-life scenario this time focusing on a single process. Students work in groups to analyse the chosen process and design a realistic process improvement solution. Both the existing and the improved processes need to be modelled using the professional process notation. While working on this project, students engage in complexities of professional enterprise process management practices, including boundary spanning, interviews with different process stakeholders, appreciative inquiry, transdisciplinary knowledge-sharing and innovation practices as well as responsible, ethical and human-centric process thinking and innovation. By engaging with a wider university community, students are will be given an opportunity to make a real contribution to process improvement in our university. Students are expected to produce a written report describing their proposed solution as well as individual contributions. The feedback will be provided in class and/or in face-to-face consultations.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Process improvement project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group report (up to 15 pages) &amp; individual contributions (up to 4 pages)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">01/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">05:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5.00pm Monday 1 June 2020              (Week 12)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">(Week 12)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design and evaluation of a process improvement solution for the given real-life problem.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this task students are expected to communicate (orally) the key aspects of their group project and share lessons learned. All members are expected to actively contribute to their group presentation in different ways (presenting the content and/or responding to on-the-spot questions). Groups are also expected to prepare and pose on-the-spot questions to other allocated groups.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Up to 10 mins</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">03/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">05:00 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5.00pm Wednesday 3 June 2020              in-class presentations (Week 12)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation of the project highlights.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">in-class presentations (Week 12)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this task students are expected to articulate and describe a minimum of four lessons learned from their own group project as well as presentations given by other groups. The lessons learned should demonstrate the acquired “learning-how-to-learn” skills which are transferable to future enterprise process management projects.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project reflections</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">up to 3 pages</Data></Cell>
