   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">49126</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Environmental Management of Land</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">There are 5 Stages: Stage 1 Select a site prior to the commencement of block 1(the size is not important, however, a large site is difficult to assess in the prescribed time frame). The site may or may not have been developed. But the work has to be original (ie you must do your own field work) and not use, for example, a documented site or one downloaded from the internet. (Please email pam.hazelton@uts.edu.au if you wish to discuss the site selection) Describe using a map, the location of the site with respect to the entire catchment and explain how it affects or is affected by other areas in the catchment. Create a resource inventory using photographs etc (for example geology, soil, landform, and vegetation). Describe the possible limitations of your chosen site if the present land use were to change. These limitations could include access, steep slopes, zoning, soil and water quality, endangered fauna and flora, bush fire hazard and local government and community concerns. Team up with a fellow student who will read the land resource inventory and constraints/limitations and make a critical evaluation of the information supplied for your proposed land use change. The questions posed by your fellow student (which must be attached to your final submission) must then be answered by you. Stage 2 Address each of these limitations. Rank the limitations and discuss how these limitations might affect the particular use for the site that you have chosen. Stage 3 Devise a Management Plan that will address all or most of these limitations so that the land use change you have chosen could be undertaken. Stage 4 Team up with a fellow student who will read the entire report and make a critical evaluation of your proposed development. The questions posed by your fellow student (which must be attached to your final submission) must then be answered by you (see Stage 5). Stage 5 Your responses to Stage 4 must be included as a separate amendment to your management plan. The entire assignment (Stages 1-5) will be submitted to the coordinator for final marking. The name of the fellow student who has assessed your work MUST be included on the front of the assignment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">75</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable</Data></Cell>
