   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48350</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Environmental and Sanitation Engineering</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to attend two lab practicals (one is to measure PH and suspended solids and the other is to measure dissolved oxygen and biochemical oxygen demand). Students are expected to undertake pre-study (Chapter 3, Section 1 of the Lecture Notes) before coming to class in order to be familiar with standard operating and analysis procedures. After lab sessions, students need to finalise, analyse and discuss the tested results through organised group meetings by themselves, as well as complete and submit lab reports by due dates. The group reports will be marked and written feedback will be given. The detailed information and guidelines for Labs 1 and 2 can be found at UTSOnline. The group reports will be marked and written feedback will be given.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Two Laboratory Practicals</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab 1 Report due on: (14/05/2020) by (11:59 PM) Lab 2 Report due on: (28/05/2020) by (11:59 PM)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab 1 Report due on: (14/05/2020) by (11:59 PM) Lab 2 Report due on: (28/05/2020) by (11:59 PM)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To learn how to measure some basic water quality parameters such as pH, suspended solids, dissolved oxygen and biochemical oxygen demand, which are important to civil engineer in determining and monitoring water quality and pollution problem in actual life. The purposes of the proposed two practical lab classes are an essential component to this subject. Through the lab work, students can gain first-hand experience in observation and manipulation as well as developing communicative skills based on the concepts learnt in the class. During the report writing and data analysis, students will gain cognitive abilities towards critical thinking and application in real professional experience.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">University guidelines will be strictly followed for all exams. This is a closed book exam. Equations and graphs may be provided without notations. The exam will be marked and the marks will be posted at UTSOlline. Feedback about common problematic questions will be provided in the class. Each student will also have opportunity in receiving individual feedback by making appointment. The detailed information about quiz can be found at UTSOnline two weeks before the quiz. This is a closed book exam.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment Date: 02/04/2020 (3:20 – 4:30 pm)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment Date: 02/04/2020 (3:20 – 4:30 pm)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To provide formative feedback so as to reflect students' learning and understanding of the subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">University guidelines will be strictly followed for all exams. The final exam is an open book exam. Equations and graphs may be provided without notations. The detailed information about Final Exam can be found at UTSOnline two weeks before the exam.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam Period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam Period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To review the topics covered and assess students’ knowledge and understanding of the subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to organise a group field trip to either a sewage treatment plant or a water treatment plant. Most treatment plants would accept students in groups; so please organise yourselves into groups. Prior to the visit, students need to undertake research and get information about the plant visited as well as prepare a list of questions you would ask the field inspector and/or operator. Please note however, the field trip report needs to be an individual effort. The report will be marked and written feedback will be given.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Treatment Plant Field Visit Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not more than 10 pages. Brochures, drawings, etc. can be additional. Please see guidlines in UTSOline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due Date: 10 Jun. 2020 by 11:59 pm through Turnitin</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due Date: 10 Jun. 2020 by 11:59 pm through Turnitin</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Similar to the laboratory, the purpose of the field trip is to enhance student learning and share new experiences that provide the opportunity for students to explore actual water and wastewater treatment processes in an authentic setting. Before the field trip, students will develop inquiry and organizational skills by working in teams. During the field trip, students can further apply learning to real scenarios and link theoretical knowledge with real engineering practice. Thus, they will gain a better understanding of the interrelationships between science and technology. Through communication with other students and the field inspector and/or operator, students will be able to build curiosity and critical thinking and problem solving skills. After the trip, students are required to complete an individual report in order to (1) provide an in-depth discussion on the findings of the trip; (2) address the differences between theoretical learning and the actual world; (3) evaluate the purpose of the treatment plant with respect to mitigation of environmental and (4) give recommendations to improve the current plant with respect to design, operation and maintenance.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
