   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">31255</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Finance and IT Professionals</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The Online Problem Sets represent self-directed online resource utilisation within a digital platform. Students are required to engage with the subject material and demonstrate mastery of technical and theoretical knowledge weekly by testing their understanding. Marks will be awarded on the level of mastery achieved prior to the commencement of each week. There will be 10 weekly Online Problem Sets undertaken during the seminar testing each student's skills developed and knowledge constructed of the subject content. These problem sets act as a feedback loop for students to gauge their progression and understanding of the material presented and highlights which learning objectives students need to improve weekly.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online Problem Sets</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week to week.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week to week.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online Problem Sets focus on financial management concepts and principles to which students must apply analytical skills and critical thinking of how finance influences businesses. They consist of a combination of multiple choice, numerical and case study questions. The aim of the Online Problem Sets is to help students develop study habits and address learning objectives of each week.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assignment is based on collaborative work among group members for the creation of a financial model for your business/ start-up. Your group is required to produce a financial model (using the guidelines available in UTSOnline) of your business. It is based on the work that has been, and is being, undertaken in the workshop, online material on UTS and other information which must be researched and /or resourcefully created. For the group assignment, students will be assessed as a team, which means each group member will normally receive the same mark. No complaints about group operation will be considered after the assignment has been submitted. Please see the assignment section of UTSOnline for full details.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group Assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submitted on Week 10 (Sunday, at 11:55pm)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submitted on Week 10 (Sunday, at 11:55pm)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to build a financial model to determine the financial health of an existing firm in addition to prepare another model for a start-up in order to determine its feasibility.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation skills are an important asset for a student’s business career. Presentations are based on and reflect industry experience and real world, contemporary issues. Each group is required to produce a 15-minute presentation with audio. The presentation is to be delivered in PowerPoint or an alternative digital visual presentation method (e.g., Presi, Keynote etc.) and contain the associated audio of the presentation content (the soundtrack).  Students will work in teams and submit a joint presentation, and will allocate the effort across the members of the group. Students are encouraged to come to an agreement on the allocation, and procedures for dealing with any disagreements should anyone not contribute. All group members must contribute to the production of the presentation, and speak on the presentation soundtrack. Assessment marks will be allocated according to both coherent analysis and presentation style. Please see the assignments section of UTSOnline for full details.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation and Screencast</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submitted on Week 10 (Sunday, at 11:55pm)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students have to present the analysis and evaluation of their case study in order to conclude the financial soundness of existing business and the feasibility of a start-up.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submitted on Week 10 (Sunday, at 11:55pm)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The entire subject content is assessed in the examination, including all work covered in workshops and online material. Students will face tasks and problems similar to what they would experience in any firm. This will require students to analyse information provided to them and use that in combination with the skills developed in the subject to complete financing/investment based tasks. The open book final exam will be 2 hours in length and will test a student's understanding of the applied finance contents presented on the subject. The exam consists of two sections; section A: multiple-choice questions and section B: short answer questions. Students will be provided with a formula sheet only. Examinations will be conducted under University examination conditions, and hence thoroughly address concerns regarding secure assessment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
