   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41084</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Fundamentals Studio A</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students create an Individual Learning Contract (ILC) and then have it agreed to by their academic mentor(s). The ILC will be created as a UTSOnline Portfolio, with a structured format and length requirements as noted in the template that will be provided. The ILC should describe the following:  Activities that will enable the student to gain the knowledge and skills that will enhance the student’s contribution to the Team and Product/Prototype. Activities that will enable the student to gain extra knowledge and skills that they nominate, and do not necessarily address the Product/Prototype. The knowledge and/or skill that they have chosen to teach to their peers.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual Learning Contract Agreement</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Creation of an Individual Learning Contract allows students to identify and document their learning direction, and set goals for the session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students must submit evidence of their individual work completed throughout the session that demonstrates how they have met the goals articulated in the Individual Learning Contract (ILC). Students' work will be assessed against what was agreed in the ILC. The length and format of the submission will depend upon what activities were chosen in the ILC. Part of this includes teaching the class activity that was agreed in the Individual Learning Contract.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual Learning Contract Finalisation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In finalizing the Individual Learning Contract, students demonstrate that they have achieved the goals set near the start of the session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Teams create a written proposal for their Product/Prototype, and have it agreed to by their Product Owner(s) and academic mentor(s). The proposal should include agreed deliverables and a timeline for the work to be carried out. The length of the proposal should be sufficient to fully describe the proposed Product, deliverables and timeline. A template with suggested section headings will be provided as guidance. Teams set up a WiKi on UTSOnline that they will use throughout the session for delivery of the Product outcomes. This assessment task (the proposal) forms the first item to be posted on the WiKi.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Team Product/Prototype Proposal</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Creating a Product/Prototype Proposal allows teams of students to define a scope for their studio work for the current session, and to negotiate the scope and outcomes with the Product Owner(s) and academic mentor(s).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">There are three parts to this task:  The working Product or Prototype, which must be demonstrated in class. A presentation delivered in class that describes what was produced, including a demonstration. The length of the presentation will typically be between 10-20 minutes depending upon the number of students in the team. A set of WiKi pages that document the Product/Prototype produced. The number of WiKi pages will depend upon the complexity of the Product/Prototype and the number of students in the team. There must be a history of regular contributions from the proposal stage to delivery, that shows the team's progress over time.  All students must be present in class for the final presentation/demonstration, and all students must have contributed to the WiKi throughout the session. The WiKi should document the process followed, and contributions by individual team members.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Team Product/Prototype Delivery</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students demonstrate their ability to deliver a Product or Prototype to an agreed scope. In doing so, students also demonstrate their capacity to solve problems, create solutions, work in teams, communicate professionally, and manage time and tasks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each student creates a Personal Design Journal and record their experiences and reflections on a regular basis. The students may use whatever tool or medium they choose to record their journal. If they do not use the Journals in UTSOnline, they must at least copy their entries to that Journal for assessment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Personal Design Journal</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">No specific length.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to Program.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to Program.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Creation of a Personal Design Journal that allows each student to record and reflect on their process and experiences in completing both their Individual Learning Contracts as well as their participation in the Team Product.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The RPF is the Relative Performance scaling factor calculated by the peer assessment tool: SPARK, based on input from you and your team members. Students rate themselves and their peers using the criteria outlined in the Criteria section. SPARK then calculates a number of factors, including an individual RPF for all designated group assessment tasks. More information about SPARK and how the RPF is calculated is contained within a separate document available on UTSOnline and is also available from the front page and other &quot;help&quot; based information of the software tool itself. Note that within SPARK, it is possible for the subject co-ordinator to exclude one or more person’s marks from RPF calculations if there is evidence of untruthful or inappropriate ratings being made. This could occur when group members collude to fabricate RPF values or from individuals providing inflated ratings. These situations are easily detected by reviewing factors generated by SPARK or by even by SPARK itself. The subject co-ordinator reserves the right to override SPARK calculated factors in such cases. Students deemed to have provided untruthful or inflated ratings will be given a low RPF which may result in failing the subject. Students who fail to provide the required ratings during the prescribed rating period shall be allocated an RPF of 0.5. Which will most likely result in a low mark for the group task and a danger of failing the subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to Program.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to Program.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual marks are determined from your group work by scaling the group marks (for each group assigment), by your individual Relative Performance Factor (RPF) as determined by peer review (using a software based peer review system) - so that your final group work assessment reflects your individual effort and ability to work in a team in developing and delivering the project materials.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
