   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48430</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Fundamentals of C Programming</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students do weekly quizzes related to Forums and Laboratory classes. Further details of the task and the assessment criteria will be made available on UTSOnline. Students receive immediate feedback and suggestions on additional learning resources.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">23:59 on Friday in Week 3-7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">23:59 on Friday in Week 3-7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reinforce and assess the understanding of basic programming concepts and fundamental C programming concepts.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students develop practical C programming skills using a problem-based approach. Data Engineers regularly collect, process and store data. In this task students will develop a deeper understanding of how C programming language can be used for collecting, processing and storing data. Further details of the task, the assessment criteria and submission requirements will be made available on UTSOnline. Students receive individual marks and feedback through UTSOnline within three weeks of submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">23:59 on Friday in Week 7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">23:59 on Friday in Week 7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assess the understanding of fundamental C programming concepts, and how they can be applied for real-world Data Engineering.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this research-inspired project, students will work together in teams to develop a professional grade program that manipulates real-world data. Students meet their team members in the laboratory sessions to research and discuss about common industry data manipulation needs such as compression, encryption and translation. Project will be implemented as an industry grade C library using header files that follow a systems approach. Students develop team working skills, project management skills and communication skills. The project has 3 group deliverables:  Project report C source code Presentation  All deliverables will receive a group mark which will be scaled as per the peer review feedback (a rating) of each individual student. UTS Spark will be used for the peer review. Further details of the task, the assessment criteria and submission requirements will be made available on UTSOnline. Students receive ongoing verbal feedback during the laboratory sessions. Individual marks and feedback for the developed program will be given through UTSOnline within three weeks of submission. No late submissions are accepted for the presentation.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project report should be no more than 10 pages. Any content in excess of the page limit will not be marked.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project report and the source code of the program – 23:59 on Friday Week 11  Presentation – during the laboratory class in week 12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assess the ability to develop and deliver programming solutions as a group.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project report and the source code of the program – 23:59 on Friday Week 11  Presentation – during the laboratory class in week 12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In the exam students will answer short questions, critique given snippets of code, explain fundamental programming concepts and professional practice, and develop programs in C language. The exam will be based on the material covered in the forums and laboratories. The final exam is restricted open book. Students are allowed to bring a single sided handwritten A4 sheet with notes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Comprehensively evaluate students' understanding of the fundamental programming concepts, the ability to apply them using the C language, and the awareness of professional coding practices.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
