   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48620</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Fundamentals of Mechanical Engineering</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Solve problems by applying fundamental mechanics principles and FBD concepts. The time allowed for a quiz is 30 minutes. The quiz will commence promptly at the scheduled lecture starting time (the first hour of the lecture). The quiz will be open book. The quiz will be marked on correctness of solution and presentation. A detailed procedure is required for full marks. The procedure includes: (1) diagrams reasonably large, clear, neat and labelled; (2) All forces and moments labelled and with arrowheads to show direction; (3) Position vectors clearly labelled; (4) All quantities have units; (5) Correct directions for action and reaction forces and couple-moments if applicable; (6) Free-body diagrams, equations of equilibrium and calculations; (7) Identification of the difference in moments of inertia about the centroidal axis and the corresponding parallel axis; (8) Application of the parallel-axis theorem; (9) Determination of internal forces using equations of equilibrium; (10) Correct shear force and bending moment functions for each segment; (11) Correct use of a coordinate system to study the motion of a particle along a curved path; and (12) Analysis of the kinematic relations for studying the dependent motion of two particles.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Five in-Class Quizzes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30 minutes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The five In-Class Quizzes will be held during the first half hour of lectures in Week 4, 6, 8, 10 &amp; 12. Refer to the Program Section for time and topic of each quiz.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The five In-Class Quizzes will be held during the first half hour of lectures in Week 4, 6, 8, 10 &amp; 12. Refer to the Program Section for time and topic of each quiz.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The five quizzes aim to develop and test students’ understanding of the fundamental principles of Newtonian mechanics, and students’ ability to apply the FBD concept with confidence. The other associated aims are to build the academic foundation for students’ professional engineering education and develop their professional communication skills.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Solve problems based on the knowledge of elementary mechanics including statics, mechanics of materials and dynamics. The final exam will be held during the normal exam period. The final exam will be an open book exam with a duration of 2 hours plus 10 minutes reading, and will be based on all material (both lectures and tutorials) covered in the subject. Students are expected to reflect on what they learned about solving mechanical problems by applying fundamental mechanics principles.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2 hours plus 10 minutes reading time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The aim of final exam is to assess students' understanding of elementary mechanics including statics, mechanics of materials and dynamics. The other aim is to lay the basis for further study in this area in later subjects.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
