   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">31103</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Game Design Studio 2</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group work to create a playable game. This is the 1st of 5 Sprints. At the end of this Sprint, you need to have a game design document and a playable tech demo demonstrating the main game mechanics. This tech demo must be playable by the users with very little instructions. Sprint Report and Game Design Document templates are provided on UTS Online and must be submitted by the ScrumMaster.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sprint 1– Game Design Document and Tech Demo</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12/08/2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">09:00 AM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">9.00am Monday 12 August 2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group work to create a playable game. This is the 2nd of 5 Sprints. You must submit a fully functioning game that can be played with and demonstrates the main game mechanics and controls. At the end of this Sprint, you need to have tested your game with at least 10 users. You need to summarise your user testing results and what changes you have made as a result of that user testing in your Sprint Report.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sprint 2 -– Playable Game with No Sounds</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">27/08/2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Tuesday 27 August 2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group work to create a polished game with novel and unique sounds and music. This is the 3rd of 5 Sprints. At the end of this Sprint, you need to have a game with rough audio assets that can be played to allow you to do further testing. The game must be playable without you having to explain the controls, the goal or the scoring system. Your game needs to have some type of game metrics to collect data must during gameplay.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sprint 3 -Playable Game with 50% of the Sounds</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">16/09/2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 16 September 2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group work to create a polished game with novel and unique sounds and music. This is the 4th of 5 Sprints. At the end of this Sprint, you need to have a game with more polished audio assets. Your game should also offer more content to play with. The game must be playable without you having to explain the controls, the goal or the scoring system. Your game needs to have some type of game metrics to collect data must during gameplay.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sprint 4 - Playable Game with 100% of the Sounds</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">01/10/2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Tuesday 1 October 2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Improve written communication and critical thinking</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is the last sprint. Students should focus on polishing and tweaking the game. The game must have polished audio assets and sufficient content to play with. Also some form of in-game data collection. All your documentation, source code, etc has to be submitted by the end of Week 12. See UTSOnline for a list of deliverables.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sprint 5 - Playable and fully functioning Game with Polished Sounds</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15/10/2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">09:00 AM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">9.00am Tuesday 15 October 2019</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual assignment to demonstrate in-depth knowledge of the latest research in games. Students will choose a research paper from FDG (Foundations of Digital Games) or DIGRA (Digital Games Research Association) conference from the last 2 years and prepare a 10 minutes poster presentation. The presentation should focus on synthesizing the research work described in the paper and should explicitly mention the key contributions of the chosen article. Also, students must discuss how this piece of research could be applied to their current (or future) game development projects. These 10 min presentations will take place in class every week. Poster presentations will start in Week-4. Time-slots will be allocated before week 3.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Research Paper Synthesis</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Signup for a slot before the beginning of Week 4 for &quot;Research Paper Synthesis&quot;. Presentations will be throughout the semester in class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Signup for a slot before the beginning of Week 4 for &quot;Research Paper Synthesis&quot;. Presentations will be throughout the semester in class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Improve communication and critical thinking.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group assignment to demonstrate in-depth knowledge of the latest unity advanced features. Students will choose an advance unity feature from the Unity documentation and will make a 10-15 minutes video tutorial on it. The video should focus on how the chosen feature can help them to make better and more polished games. The tutorial should include a practical example. These 10 min videos will be played in during the labs in Weeks 4,5,7,9,11. This will be peer-marked during the lab. *** Video tutorial presentations will start in Week-4. Time-slots will be allocated before week 3. Best tutorials will be played at the student showcase and will be published on the UTS Games Studio website (if students agree).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unity Advanced Feature Tutorial</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Signup for a slot before the beginning of Week 4 for &quot;Advanced Unity Feature Tutorial&quot;. Presentations will be throughout the semester in the Lab.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Signup for a slot before the beginning of Week 4 for &quot;Advanced Unity Feature Tutorial&quot;. Presentations will be throughout the semester in the Lab.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Improve communication and critical thinking.</Data></Cell>
   </Row>
   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">32004</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Game Development</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual work on lab exercises. The weekly lab exercises are designed to instruct students of fundamental development techniques, while requiring students to perform self-directed study and demonstrate problem solving for higher marks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Programming Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable              Weekly lab activities are to be submitted on Canvas by Monday 9am of the following week. Students must also complete and submit the included assessment deceleration for each week, indicating the progress that was made.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly lab activities are to be submitted on Canvas by Monday 9am of the following week. Students must also complete and submit the included assessment deceleration for each week, indicating the progress that was made.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will be graded individually on their code submissions.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is an individual in-class, open book quiz based on content taught in class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 10              during lab time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">during lab time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will be graded individually and marks are allocated per question as will be specified in the quiz.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment requires students to apply what they have learned to recreate a classic 2D game. Students will be able to choose form a small list of games to create, drawn from the early eras of the games industry. At this stage, the project will need to have the scene for a single level set up, visual assets and animations created, and basic player movement functionality implemented. Students will also need to provide a design for a new twist on the game that is to be delivered upon in Assessment 4. Additional assessment details and marking rubric will provided on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Classic Game Recreation - Getting Started</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">21/09/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 21 September 2020              Submission through Git - commit and push to new branch called &quot;Assess3&quot;. Further instructions on submission procedure in the specifications provided on Canvas.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will be graded on their ability to apply the knowledge from the lectures, labs, and self-directed study to establish the foundations for recreating a classic 2D game.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submission through Git - commit and push to new branch called &quot;Assess3&quot;. Further instructions on submission procedure in the specifications provided on Canvas.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment extends upon Assessment 3 by requiring students to produce two scenes in their recreated game. The first is to as faithful of a recreation of a level from the classic game as possible, demonstrating the students ability to interpret an existing game experience and develop it. The second scene is to contain the student's own innovative twist (as promised in Assessment 3) to the core game mechanics and player experience, which exposes students to a design and development cycle of original content. Students are encouraged to push the boundaries of their skill in this second scene and seek new knowledge in order to implement their novel gameplay design. Additional assessment details and marking rubric will provided on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Classic Game Recreation - Enhancing and Innovating</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">35</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">23/10/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Friday 23 October 2020              Submission through Git - commit and push to new branch called &quot;Assess4&quot;. Further instructions on submission procedure in the specifications provided on Canvas.</Data></Cell>
