   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41189</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Introduction to Human-centred Complex Systems</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In groups, students will choose one of a number of given themes (such as Smart Cities, IoT, Social impacts of technology, etc.). They will undertake research to identify a complex system within that theme and problem within that system. Analyse the system’s characteristics such as its elements and its possible functions and discuss why the system may be classified as a human-centred complex system. Use any of the concepts or tools covered in the subject to capture the details of the selected problem within their system – the factors that influence it, the impacts of the problem if unresolved, the inter-relationships between the influencing factors, and so on. Identify possible ways to redress the problem and suggest a recommended way. Analyse the implications of the group’s preferred interventions on stakeholders and on other systems. Students will work as a group to produce a relevant report (30% marks) and submit an individual reflection on the project (20%).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">4000 words maximum (not including cover page, table of contents, diagrams and references)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Allow students to collaboratively engage with complex systems and develop and demonstrate their understanding of such systems.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students make weekly contributions to their portfolio, demonstrating the development of their thinking in this area and their engagement with the issues discussed in class. Students develop their learning in the area of complex systems and demonstrate this through evidence of inquiry and reflection. Through peer communication and reflection students synthesize their learning throughout the session to build a holistic picture of the subject matter.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflective portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate evidence of enquiry and reflection on complex systems.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is evidenced by the contribution by the student, and by their provision of, constructive feedback on peer work. Exercises, topics and situations will be posed for students to comment on and debate. 5 sets of discussion/debates will be spread throughout the session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online engagement</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
