   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">32524</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">LANS and Routing</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment is composed of two equally-weighted tasks: Part I, an individual task, where each student is to subnet (with VLSM) an IPv4 network to meet specified requirements; Part II, a group-based, hands-on/practical task, where each group is to build a small network using allocated hardware and do basic configurations to achieve end-to-end connectivity.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mid-session Assessment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 6 Tutorial time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 6 Tutorial time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This early session assessment is designed to give students early feedback so that students can adjust their learning strategy and improve their learning efficiency.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment task is a comprehensive project that covers the main topics for this subject. It is to be completed as a group activity with 80 percent of total marks being awarded individually. The task involves designing and testing a small to medium sized network based on the requirements specified in the Case Study document that will be issued in Week 3. Groups are to read the requirements, and design and test the network progressively as the topics required are covered during lab sessions. The Case Study will be assessed as a group-based assignment and consists of the following two-stage submissions:  Part A: IPv4 Addressing Milestone Submission (20%): Each group as a whole are required to submit their IPv4 addressing scheme to their instructor in their Week 5 class session for feedback. Part B: Case Study Lab Demonstration on Packet Tracer (80%): Individual students are to implement their designed network on Packet Tracer in their Week 10 class. Students will then test and mark each other’s work based on given marking criteria on site.  It is worth of noting that the workload for properly completing this assignment is not trivial. The tasks required in the Final Skills Based Assessment will come from this assignment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case Study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Part A: by the end of the Week 6 Tutorial class; Part B: during Week 10 Tutorial</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Part A: by the end of the Week 6 Tutorial class; Part B: during Week 10 Tutorial</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">As an authentic, comprehensive assignment, this assessment task provides an opportunity for students to integrate individual topics covered in each week into a real network.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is a hands-on/practical assessment based on group work. However, around 80 percent of marks are awarded to items on which each student in a group is assessed individually. Given network specifications and detailed tasks, each group will be given around one hour to build a small to medium sized network on real equipment allocated by instructors. Students are to apply their knowledge and skills to cable the network, configure devices, and troubleshoot if necessary, to demonstrate the connectivity and functionality of their network. Students' hand-written Engineering Journals/notes may be allowed to use subject to instructors' inspection.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Skills-based Assessment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">25</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12 Class time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">As a final practical assessment, this assesment task evaluates students' competency on their hands-on skills.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12 Class time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This examination is a restricted open book individual assessment held during the exam period at the end of the session, where students are allowed to use one double-sided A4-page of handwritten notes. Each student will be given two hours to complete up to ten short-answer questions in writing (most questions contain sub-questions). The questions will be based on the key concepts presented during weekly lectures, as well as labs, during the whole session. Sample questions will be made available toward the end of the session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Written Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
