    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">You should take this optional online quiz to ensure you have read and understood critical items from the subject outline, for this subject, before proceeding with further studies in this subject. Please ensure you have read the Subject Outline before commencing the Quiz. You may attempt this quiz multiple times - until you are satisfied with the outcome. Please note, this a voluntary quiz,meant for your development within the subject but earns you no assessable marks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz 1</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The quiz is marked by UTSOnline and the results are provided immediately upon completion of the quiz.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The intent of this assessment task is to encourage you to read the subject outline and to ensure you understand it.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Explore, Discover, and Report: A brief investigation of information tools and information resources currently utilized within your chosen engineering discipline. This is an individual assignment with the end result strictly limited to 8 physical pages of assessable text, supporting diagrams and tables (typical range: 3000 word min - 6000 word max).  Any report exceeding the 8 physical page limit will be penalised severely - this is an exercise in stakeholder identification (who the report is written for), analysing and conforming to the specification shown as follows. The purpose of this assignment is to explicitly expose you to the sort of information technologies employed by practitioners of engineering, within your chosen sub-discipline (e.g. Civil, Mechanical, Industrial, etc.). A secondary purpose is to familiarise and remind you of correct academic conduct at UTS. The primary purpose is that you investigate a declared engineering branch/sub-discipline which is the focus of your studies or employment, on behalf of a stakeholder (whom you are expected to analyse and identify). If you have a reason for doing otherwise, such as exploring other career options, you may choose and declare an engineering discipline other than that of your major/job. Given time and report size constraints, it is not reasonable to expect an exhaustive consideration of this topic but you are expected to analyse, determine any logical ordering and include:  A brief definition of information technology, written in your own words. A brief overview/description of the engineering sub-discipline which you have chosen as the focus of your effort and  briefly distinguish the various type of knowledge work requiring information technology support, e.g. management, design, field work, construction, manufacturing, decision support, continual learning, etc.   A minimum of two (2) telephone, in-person, or email interviews with practitioners (grab the phone book if you do not know any personally).  One (1) or more should be in management roles. One (1) or more should be in engineering roles.   Summaries of at least four (4) papers specifically addressing some use of information technology in your chosen engineering sub-discipline that you find through UTSLibraryOnline:  Two (2) papers obtained via IEEEXplore, the ACM Digital Library, or other scholarly engineering databases that discuss technology adoption, in your chosen and declared engineering sub-discipline.  Papers must have been published within the last 10 years.   Two (2) papers obtained via IEEEXplore, the ACM Digital Library, or other scholarly engineering databases that discuss organisational transformation, as a result of the identified and discussed technology adoption (see above), within your chosen and declared engineering sub-discipline.  Papers must have been published within the last 10 years.     A compilation of information technology tools and information technology resources that are significant to practitioners (managers and engineers) of your chosen engineering sub-discipline:  Information Technology Tools may include:  Design, Analysis, Synthesis, Testing Modelling and Simulation CAD/CAM Project Management Collaboration Documentation management Quality assurance and control Cost estimation Bid/Proposal generation Wireless devices   Information Technology Resources should include:  Information repositories / portals Regulatory bodies Standards bodies Professional organisations     Present the compilation in a table format. Each table row should include the items: name, description, reference source, and classification (according to the type of knowledge work it supports). Note: the reference source is where to find the resource – NOT who told you about it. Finally analyse, synthesize, internalize, and summarize what was learned in this exercise. It must focus on the results of your research, interviews, tables, formal paper summaries, definitions plus other researched material as described above and comprise the bulk of the report that you submit (about 50% in logical effort, overall). Before submitting the report, the Academic Conduct Quiz (see Assessment Tasks, UTSOnline) must be successfully complete, else the Turnitin link for submitting the report will not be accessible.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1 (individual)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">35</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Maximum 3000-6000 words (8 physical pages of assessable text, maximum).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">refer to Program</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The assessment sheet used to mark and review your assignment (including a detailed breakdown of the marks assigned) is available for viewing on UTSOnline. Click on the view/submit link for this assignment and then click on the rubric symbol. Submissions will be checked using the ‘turnitin’ software, which is embedded within UTSOnline. A short time after submitting your assignment (typically 10 minutes or so - but be prepared for 24 hours or more in periods of heavy loading) you will be able to view the originality report for your assignment, complete with an overall matching index. The matching index for your submission must be less than 25%. You can submit multiple times prior to the submission deadline to ensure that this requirement is met. Submissions contravening this requirement shall be summarily reported to the Faculty for further investigation.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The principal intent of this assessment task is to encourage you to explore, discover, reflect and report succinctly on those IT tools and resources as used or required by practitioners in your chosen engineering sub-discipline. A further intent is to expose you to a user specification, encourage you to comply with that specification; identify the logical ordering of material to be included in your report; maintain professional discipline when compiling the report; focus on the quality of process/product; and importantly, identify the report's ultimate stakeholder.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">You will be assigned to a group of approximately 4-5 students (in some semesters it might be as large as 6). You will need to communicate with each other and your &quot;customer&quot; by producing a group web page that provides a proposal for an achievable group IT project (to be resolved in Assignment 2b) and details of your background - as a group and as individuals. The proposal should identify the stakeholder/s and address the following:  A brief background of each group member (including a photo) and a summary of the topic area that each group member wrote about in their first assignment; Why you chose that topic, and briefly, what you learnt from doing the first assignment (i.e. a brief reflection); An achievable, proposed topic for the group project report (see Assessment Task 4) that takes into account the backgrounds of each of the group members and topics that were addressed in the first assignment. The proposed topic should be related in some way to individual topics as discussed in the individual Assignment 1s. A justification of why you collectively chose this topic based on the background and experience of each of the group members; What you expect you gain from completing the group project (i.e. a set of group learning goals).  Please refer to the information below about assignment 2b to guide you in the choice of topic for this task. Your group web page may be constructed using any IT tool you like. Applications most commonly used by students for tasks like this include:  Microsoft Frontpage Microsoft Word (use the “save as” menu and select the html option) Various freeware web page authoring tools – search for them on the web  Your submission must be submitted as a zipped file (.zip) that contains all necessary files, contained within a single directory and must open and display correctly using Internet Explorer 7.0 (or better) and/or Mozilla Firefox - these are the only supported web browsers. The only supported compression software (zipped files) is .zip - no other compression format can or will be &quot;unzipped&quot;. Note: while the appearance of your web page is not being assessed, it must be laid out neatly, logically and clearly; addresses all details as shown above and is &quot;customer/stakeholder focussed&quot;. The content is what counts, so do not spend time making it look pretty - although the submission must be neat and clean and look professional. A readme on how the webpage is &quot;run&quot; must be included in the .zip file. The submitted webpage must work on any UTS computer. A webpage that does not &quot;run&quot; or is deemed not complete by the marker, may earn zero marks. The name of your submitted zip file shall use the format: Ass2a_group_X where X is your group name. The file must include a completed and signed Faculty of Engineering and IT declaration of originality. Submissions not meeting these requirements may have marks deducted or not marked at all (as in the case of any other format than .zip). As a precaution, a copy of the .zip file (the submission) should be placed in the Group file exchange area.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 2a: Group web page proposal</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to Program.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The assessment sheet used to mark and review your ass2a submission (including a detailed breakdown of the assigned group marks) is available for viewing on UTSOnline. Click on the link to submit your assignment and then click on &quot;View Rubric&quot;.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To get started on the group project and provide an opportunity for feedback on the proposed topic for the group project, to be finalised in the Group project report. Also for students in the group to get to know each other and their area of engineering; and to provide a mechanism for effective and productive collaboration..</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is a major group-based project assignment. Each group has to conduct research and submit a report (around 40 pages or 12,000-30,000 words). The report is to take the form of a detailed feasibility study and project plan for the introduction of a significant IT system (an “information architecture”) into a engineering organisation to address a specific set of needs, as proposed in Assignment 2a (Assessment Item 3). As a guide, the report should include at least three major parts: 1. Definition of the project, 2. Research into the project and 3. Project Analysis - any deviation from this structure should be identified and explained in an introduction to the report. The type of IT system and type of engineering organisation that your group collectively chooses must be based on the backgrounds and experiences of the group members and defined in Assignment 2a. If you were unable to do this successfully, for whatever reason, you could choose an engineering faculty in a virtual University, say, as the engineering organisation and an IT-based subject or course as your target for the information architecture. You might even decide to use this subject (49013) as your target project! In any event the feasibility study's topic must be previously approved by the subject's coordinator or Assignment 2a marker. You are free to use any sources of information to help you with this project, however, these should be fully referenced using the Harvard UTS referencing guide (see UTS Library link). It is expected you make use of the scholarly literature on IT you accessed in Assignment 1 to guide your design decisions in this project. You should also pay attention to anticipated impacts of your IT system on the engineering organisation. The final report should include the following elements (tables, forms, charts, etc.) as minimum requirements:  Customer Needs Analysis (Market Survey) Literature Review Feasibility Study Impact Analysis Project Schedule Responsibility Matrix Work Breakdown Structure (Gantt Chart) Project Cost Estimation  Capital Puchase Installation &amp; Commissioning Defect Management/Repair Maintenance Training   Quality Control Plan Risk Management Plan Glossary of Terms  It is up to the group how to select the most appropriate structure for its final report. However, the following has been provided as a guide only:  Abstract Introduction Methodology Discussion Conclusion References  Your group’s assignment will be assessed by the Instructor and another group. Both assessments will be used (as equal weighting) to establish an averaged grade for your assignment. As an example, suppose your assignment was peer assessed by another group and awarded a mark of 28 out of 40, whereas the assignment marker awarded your group a grade of 32/40. Your averaged mark, which is the one recorded and used for the calculation of your final mark and grade in the subject would be 30/40. Where a peer assessment from another group is deemed to be inappropriately high, the subject co-ordinator may decide to use the assignment marker awarded mark alone.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 2b: Group project report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to Program.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The assessment sheet used to mark and review your ass2b submission (including a detailed breakdown of the assigned group marks) is available for viewing on UTSOnline. Click on the link to submit your assignment and then click on &quot;View Rubric&quot;.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Gain experience in the design of an information architecture to address a specific need within a chosen engineering organisation as identified by the &quot;proposed topic&quot;, in Assessment Task 3. Also, to obtain experience in developing a detailed information technology, feasiblity study and project plan, which is at a higher level than a detailed, solution study/plan of the implementation of the specific need.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this assessment task, each group will be provided with the Assessment 2b report of another group participating in the subject this semester. Your group will then assess the work of the other group according to the assessment criteria for Assignment 2b (Assessment item 4). Your submission must have detailed comments about each section of the report with an evidence-based assessment as per the criteria. This means that you should provide references to page numbers and/or paragraph numbers as evidence of meeting each criterion. It is expected that this is a detailed assessment with detailed comments on each section, indicating what has been done well and what needs improvement. Where an important item has been identified as needing improvement, the peer assessment may include a suggestion of exactly how it could be improved (for example, to provide a sample paragraph or section that does meet the stated criteria). Your groups peer assessment should be around 8 pages in length (about 3000 words). You must provide two copies of your ass2c submission – one with a declaration of originality included with your names and student numbers of all group members and an originality report (with overall matching index) obtained by checking the report using one of the optional links to Turnitin; the other copy with no names or references to the assessing group or any other identifying features to the assessors - filename Ass2C Group X.pdf, where X is your group name. This second copy will be given to the group you are assessing as feedback. Please upload one zip file to UTSOnline for ass2c containing the 2 files. The de-identified copy should be called “feedback from group X .pdf” where X is the name of the assessed group. Note the zip file name should clearly identify the submitting group. You will then be assessed (by the instructor) on the completeness, appropriateness and quality of your peer assessment and awarded a mark out of 10. This will be weighted by the RPF of each group member for inclusion into the final subject assessment. Peer assessment of the major group project shall be treated seriously - professionally. Groups who award unrealistically high marks to other groups will be marked down severely for their Assignment 2c, which may result in your group failing the subject while the other group may pass. Similarly, your marks should not be too low either! SUBMISSION REQUIREMENTS  You must submit two softcopy versions of your ass2c packaged in a .zip file name to identify the submitting group (e.g. Ass2C_X.pdf - where X is your group name):  Version 1: Filename: Ass2C Group X.pdf where X is your group name which includes your Ass2c, signed declaration of originality with student names and numbers and an originality report (with overall matching index) obtained by checking the report using one of the optional links to Turnitin. Version 2: Filename feedback from group X.pdf where X is your group name with no declaration of originality and with no names or student numbers on it in any part of the submission (de-identified). Be sure to not include your name, student numbers, group name or any other identifying item anywhere in the document including title page, text, headers &amp; footers and also in the document properties area. This file will be sent to another group for peer assessment. Both versions must be zipped together (.zip, only) and submitted via the: Group Project Peer Assessment (ass2c) link, in the Assessment Tasks folder of UTSOnline. A copy of the .zip file shall be maintained by File Exchange area of your group on UTSOnline. Failure to comply with these requirements will result in marking penalties and if any other compression software format than the defined .zip is used, the assignment shall not be marked.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 2c: Group project peer assessment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">around 3000 words (8 pages)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">refer to Program</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The RPF is the Relative Performance scaling factor calculated by SPARK, based on input from you and your group members. Students rate themselves and their peers using the criteria outlined in the Criteria section. SPARK then calculates a number of factors, including an individual RPF for all designated group assessment tasks. More information about SPARK and how the RPF is calculated is contained within a separate document available on UTSOnline and is also available from the front page and other &quot;help&quot; based information of the software tool itself. Note that within SPARK, it is possible for the subject co-ordinator to exclude one or more person’s marks from RPF calculations if there is evidence of untruthful or inappropriate ratings being made. This can occur from group members colluding to fabricate RPF values or from individuals providing inflated ratings. These situations are easily detected by reviewing factors generated by SPARK or by even by SPARK itself. The subject co-ordinator reserves the right to override SPARK calculated factors in such cases. Students deemed to have provided untruthful or inflated ratings will be given a low RPF which may result in failing the subject. Students who fail to provide the required ratings during the prescribed rating period shall be allocated an RPF of 0.5. Which will most likely result in a low mark for the group task and a danger of failing the subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RPF - Relative Performance Factor</Data></Cell>
