   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">49316</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Materials Handling</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The storage bin, silo or hopper is one of the most important items of equipment in any bulk solids handling installation, a poorly flowing hopper can have consequences extending throughout the plant. Improper design of the bins and hopper can result in various flow problems, such as those described generally as ‘arching’ or ‘rat-holing’. The flow problems lead to an overall poor performance of the storage and handling system. In this assessment, the students will practise the fundamentals of bin and hopper design based on Jenike, Janssen and Johanson design methodologies. Three calculation based questions of practical design procedure where students will need to do the following design steps(but not limited to): 1- Calculation of Simi included angle and the flow factor 2- Calculation of critical applied stresses 3- Calculation of minimum outlet diameter and 4- Calculation of discharge rate.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Bins and Hopper Design</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">14</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 4: 11.59 pm Sunday 5 April 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 4: 11.59 pm Sunday 5 April 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Introductory design project involving the design of a bin or hopper.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test work and demonstrations involving the determination and application of standard bulk solid properties will be conducted during this lab session. This will include flow properties (e.g. compressibility, wall friction) and the processing of flow property data. Students need to observe each experiment and record all relevant data and any additional items requested on the lab sheet. Students submit a completed lab report with calculations and discussion.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 6: 11.59 pm Sunday 19 April 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 6: 11.59 pm Sunday 19 April 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory experimental work</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment task number 6 is a calculation based homework assignment consists of three questions:  The Analysis of “On Going” sampling and weighing system. The analysis of Automated guided vehicle systems. The design and analysis of discrete material handling with Autonomous Mobile Robots known as the AGV (Automated guided vehicle). The analysis of automated storage/retrieval system. The students are required to determine the throughput rate of the AS/RS. Bulk materials handling innovative design project involving the construction of a design model based on basic principles.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Discrete handling</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59 pm Sunday 3 May 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design project involving the design of a Sampling and measurement systems and the evaluation of AGV system.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59 pm Sunday 3 May 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hydraulic conveying of bulk solids, or ‘slurry transport’, involves the conveyance of solid particles in suspension in a moving liquid. High-tonnage, long-distance transportation of coal, iron, copper, phosphate, limestone and various other minerals in hydraulic pipelines is now a well-established commercial alternative to other modes of bulk solids transport such as lorries, railway trains, and barges. In this course, the slurry pumping design according to Warman Slurry Pumping Handbook will be thoroughly covered. The students' design ability will be assets accordingly. Assessment 3 is homework task consists of one question: a standard slurry pumping design case requires the calculation of: 1. Pulp properties, 2. Pipe selection, 3. Slurry velocity, 4. Frictional pressure loss, 5. Limiting settling velocity, 6. Singular pressure drop, 7. Total dynamic head, 8. Pump selection and 9. Pump power.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Pneumatic and Hydraulic conveying</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">18</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 9: 11.59 pm Sunday 17 May 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 9: 11.59 pm Sunday 17 May 2020. Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Taking a systems approach to the design of complete materials handling system, e.g. a cement distribution system involving several items of bulk handling and storage equipment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assessment task number 6 is a calculation based homework assignment consists of three questions: 1- The Analysis of “On Going” sampling and weighing system. 2- The analysis of Automated guided vehicle systems. The design and analysis of discrete material handling with Autonomous Mobile Robots known as the AGV (Automated guided vehicle). 3- The analysis of automated storage/retrieval system. The students are required to determine the throughput rate of the AS/RS. 4. Bulk materials handling innovative design project involving the construction of a design model based on basic principles.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Bucket Elevators, Screw Conveyors and Belt Conveyers</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">16</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12: 11.59 pm Sunday 1 June 2020.Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12: 11.59 pm Sunday 1 June 2020.Online submission.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Introductory design project involving the assessment of needs and the design of a materials handling system such as a screw conveyor or bucket elevator.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The final exam is a 2 hour open-book exam covering lectures, tutorials and guest presentations. University approved calculators are allowed. The examination tests analytical and critical thinking and general understanding of the course material in a controlled fashion. Questions may be drawn from any aspect of the course. Marks will be assigned according to the correctness of the responses.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
