   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48670</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mechanical and Mechatronic Design</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The projects available in this project-based subject are real. They are actual projects competitively on offer from various research centres in UTS as well as external partners from industry. Although this is not the focus of this subject, your success in your project will somewhat be shaped by the match of your skill-set to that which the project requires. This assessment requires you (in your team) to prepare a convincing pitch that you will deliver to your potential ‘employer/client’ via email and during a followup interview. The ‘employer/client’ will then ‘hire’ the ‘right’ team for the job.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Interview and Pitch</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Demonstration</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 2; see Further information</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Marks will be awarded according to your ability to attract a &quot;project-offer&quot; from your team's provisional project/client. This assessment will be further detailed during the Week 2 class. Your pitch (via email) should be short and to the point. You should introduce yourselves, your general area of formal training (your degree), and your specific area of excellence within this area (the subject stream/s at which you excel). You should then highlight any relevant experience and skills you possess pertaining to the project on offer, along with providing any supporting evidence. Your interview with the client requires that you present yourself professionally, convey your ability to complete the project, and convince the client to offer you the project. Additionally, you should have questions prepared to help you determine the project requirements and what is needed from your team to complete it. Lastly, you should negotiate with the client the scope of the project so that you and your team can successfully deliver.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly one-page documents (extra pages allowed for appendices) designed to keep your client/boss informed on:  What happened during the last week, Your thoughts and recommendations on those events and Your current short-term plan.  Reports will also be required to include key milestone artifacts in certain weeks, for example a &quot;project specification in week 4&quot; or &quot;prototype in week 6&quot;. These artifacts and when they are expected will be provided in the assessment description. The reports need to summarise the meaningful progress made by the team during the previous week. They should also highlight the individual contributions of each team member. The progress report should be concise in general, but significant events should be presented in more detail. An example of a general point would be: Proved design option #2 to be non-feasible due to part X (or equivalent part) not being available. An example of a point that would warrant more detail would be: Agreed on the deliverables with the client... you would then go on to list each of them and the respective deliverables dates.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Progress Reports</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly reports are to be written starting week 3 up until week 12. Each weekly progress report is to be submitted for assessment before the tutorial class each week.  Teams will be required to present and discuss their progress reports in class.See also Further information.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly reports are to be written starting week 3 up until week 12. Each weekly progress report is to be submitted for assessment before the tutorial class each week.  Teams will be required to present and discuss their progress reports in class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Marks will be awarded according to the effectiveness with which the report presents:  What happened in the last week; Your thoughts and recommendations on those events; and Your current short-term plan.  Contribution of individual team members will be assessed, and on this grades will be allocated to individual students.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment involved two short sit-down presentations in which your group provides a brief account of your progress up until this point, and your plans for completing the project. Students will also be involved in peer assessment, being required to assess and provide constructive criticism on the work of other students.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design Review</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Will be performed in class during week 5 and week 8. Students are required to be available at these times. Students will be allocated a time within the lecture or tutorial classes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This will be marked against the details of the project/client needs, their translation into engineering requirements/project-specifications, your ideas for potential solutions (and/or solution elements) and the evaluations that have been conducted on these ideas (along with the mechanisms/tools used by the team in order to collect, elicit, compare, refine, shape, test and select the aforementioned) that were presented. The groups are expected to demonstrate a thorough engagement with the content of the classes up until this point and under the context of their project. Questions will be asked based on the subject content up until this point. Individual students will also be assessed on their peer assessment. This is based on engagement during the design review, questions asked and the assessment made. The design reviews are assessed as a group, with SPARK+ used to scale individual marks. SPARK+ (round 1) is due before Design Review 1 (week 5). Likewise, SPARK+ (round 2) is due before Design Review 2 (week 8). Failure to complete SPARK+ before the design review will result in that student receiving zeros marks for that design review.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Will be performed in class during week 5 and week 8. Students are required to be available at these times. Students will be allocated a time within the lecture or tutorial classes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A poster presentation of your project to a general audience. Specifically, the presentation should cover the motivation for the project (the needs of the client), how the design process unfolded during the project, the design choices/decisions that were made along with the appropriate rationale, your outcomes, your recommendations based on the project outcomes and the client's level of satisfaction with those outcomes and the team in general. The poster presentation will be held during a poster session that somewhat resembles a &quot;trade show&quot;, with multiple teams presenting to assessors, peers and invited guests who walk the floor examining posters and asking questions. You will also be required to submit a soft copy of your poster online once presentations have been completed. Students will also be involved in peer assessment, being required to assess and provide constructive criticism on the work of other students.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Poster Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class in Week 12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This will be marked against your team's presentation of: the motivation for the project, how the design process unfolded during the project, the design choices/decisions that were made, your outcomes, your recommendations based on the outcomes of you project, the client's level of satisfaction with those outcomes and your presentation style in general. You will be required to answer on the spot questions from assessors about your project. The poster presentation is assessed as a group, with SPARK+ (round 3) used to scale individual marks. SPARK+ (round 3) is due before the poster presentation. Failure to complete SPARK+ on time will result in that student receiving zeros marks for the poster presentation assessment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A video report (less than 5 minutes in length) that details the project. The report should cover how the design process unfolded during your project. The video report (which will somewhat resemble a video journal) should be pitched at a fellow engineer but should be assessable by your client. It should contain enough detail that a colleague could rely on it as a guide if they were to do a similar project.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period              Due 11:59pm Friday of the first week of the exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due 11:59pm Friday of the first week of the exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">You should justify your design choices and detail your engineering solutions. Your report must demonstrate that you team:  Maximised your contribution to your client by seeing unspoken needs; rather than ‘just being told what to do’ Worked to identify the core of the client's need, extracted and identified the design objectives, formed a set of evaluation criteria and a set of deliverables Effectively performed testing and developed prototypes when appropriate Communicated effectively throughout the project to your boss, client and within your team  The report is assessed as a group, with SPARK+ (round 3) used to scale individual marks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this subject your project is a real world engineering project, required to solve a real problem for a real client. You are expected to deliver what was originally negotiated with the client at the start of the project, both in terms of timing and quality. As is the case in authentic engineering practice, assessment of this task is based on the satisfaction of your client upon final project delivery. Note that the deliverables you give to the client are different from those you submit during other assessment tasks. A client may ask for a copy of your poster or your video report, but the main deliverables are something extra such as a physical product, technical report, manufacturing drawings, etc. The exact deliverables will differ depending on the project and should be agreed upon with the client in the early stages of the project.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Client Delivery</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project deliverable(s) are expected to be handed over to the client during the first week of exams (however earlier delivery is fine). The latest day for submission is Friday of the first week of the exam period. Negotiations with the client should be held ahead of time to ensure delivery is performed before this date.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project deliverable(s) are expected to be handed over to the client during the first week of exams (however earlier delivery is fine). The latest day for submission is Friday of the first week of the exam period. Negotiations with the client should be held ahead of time to ensure delivery is performed before this date.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Marks are awarded as follows:  Your team's abilty to set and manage deliverables. This involves understanding your own capabilities and available resources and then negotiating with the client a set of achievable deliverables that satisfies their wants, and to then deliver. This will be marked somewhat subjectively. Specifically, the client will be contacted to determine if the team delivered as promised (both in terms of timing and quality) AND that delivered will be compared against that promised and detailed during the teams previous assessment tasks. Interaction. Again this will be marked subjectively. Your client will be contacted and asked to rate (by their own standards) your ability to interact. Simply put, they will be asked to rate on a scale 'Would you be happy to work with them again?  Final project delivery is assessed as a group, with SPARK+ (round 3) used to scale individual marks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
