   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48622</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mechatronics 1</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this individual task, students are required to design combinational and sequential logic circuits then schematically program the FPGA using simulations. The demonstration needs to achieve a particular set of functional requirements that are detailed and communicated via UTSOnline. The final demonstration will be given in class to an assessor and requires students to answer various questions related to their design and their strategy for solving the task. Students will then write a reflections report detailing their thought process, design, successes and challenges faced. This assessment task is staged so students can complete and demonstrate each part in incremental steps. Further details about the deadlines are provided in the program and more details about the requirements and deliverables will be provided via UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1: FPGA demonstration</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Demonstration</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">25</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1 part A is due in the tutorial class Week 3; Assignment 1 Part B code submission and demonstration registration is due on Sunday of Week 5 at 21:00;  Assignment 1 part B (demonstration) is due in tutorial class Week 6; Assignment 1 part C (report) is due on Friday Week 6 at 21:00</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 1 part A is due in the tutorial class Week 3; Assignment 1 Part B code submission and demonstration registration is due on Sunday of Week 5 at 21:00;  Assignment 1 part B (demonstration) is due in tutorial class Week 6; Assignment 1 part C (report) is due on Friday Week 6 at 21:00</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To demonstrate needs to achieve a particular set of functional requirements to solve a particular problem.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In this individual task, the student is required to configure and program the MCU Microcontroller and demonstrate that it achieves a particular set of functional requirements that will be detailed and communicated via UTSOnline. The final demonstration will be given in class to an assessor and will require students answer various questions related to their software design, electronics circuitry, MCU configuration and their strategy for solving the task. Students will then write a report reflecting upon how they engineered their solution. This assessment task is staged so students can complete and demonstrate each part incrementally. Further information about the deadlines is provided in the program and more details about the requirements and deliverables will be communicated via UTSOnline. Alternative Assessment Task option for Assessment 2 Some students may be keen to participate in the &quot;Unleashed Studio Program&quot; . In this case such students will be required to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) via an &quot;Unleashed Application Form&quot;. The application will be a form on which students agree to:  Writing a new Learning Contract with agreed upon deliverables Co-designing a marking rubric (which substitutes Assessment 1 and/or Assessment 2 Parts B and C. Weighting of task will need to be adjusted accordingly and as required. Submit and demonstrate said deliverables Agree to and adhere by agreed due date. Adhere to rights and responsibilities   Rights:  Design own assignment as per above point 1 Have own Teams discussion board to collaborate with others and communicate immediately with tutors Can borrow specialised equipment (if available) from UTS research labs, clubs or centres Have greater exposure to the UTS robotics research centres.  Responsibilities:  Independent research for your own personal task/implementation Regular communication of your progress and challenges to the entire 'Unleashed' team Willingness to receive feedback and demonstrate how you have acted on that feedback Document own work (Note for Part c: you are required to write a reflection and this documentation can logically form a major part of this reflection) Work safely and responsibly with all borrowed equipment in all locations Create a short video to introduce other students to your work.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 2: MCU programming task</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Demonstration</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">25</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 2 Part A demonstration is due in tutorial class Week 9;  Assignment 2 Part B code submission and demonstration registration is due on Sunday of Week 11 at 21:00;  Assignment 2 Part B demonstration in tutorial class Week 12;  Assignment 2 Part C report is due on Friday Week 12 at 21:00.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment 2 Part A demonstration is due in tutorial class Week 9;  Assignment 2 Part B code submission and demonstration registration is due on Sunday of Week 11 at 21:00;  Assignment 2 Part B demonstration in tutorial class Week 12;  Assignment 2 Part C report is due on Friday Week 12 at 21:00.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To configure and program the MCU Microcontroller and demonstrate that it achieves a particular set of functional requirements.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will individually sit four in-class review quizzes in the weeks indicated in the program. These quizzes will assess the student’s knowledge on the topics of combinational logic circuits, sequential logic circuits, and MCU microcontrollers, in an incremental manner. The quizzes are online and will be attempted for the first time in class and will thus provide an immediate feedback opportunity to students at key times throughout the session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Review Quizzes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz 1: Week 4, in interactive lecture class; Quiz 2: Week 7, in interactive lecture class; Quiz 3: Week 10, in interactive lecture class; Quiz 4: Week 12, in interactive lecture class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Gauge own level of understanding of topics used to inform projects.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz 1: Week 4, in interactive lecture class; Quiz 2: Week 7, in interactive lecture class; Quiz 3: Week 10, in interactive lecture class; Quiz 4: Week 12, in interactive lecture class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The final exam is a centrally conducted examination that assesses a student's theoretical knowledge of combinational logic circuits, sequential logic circuits, and MCU microcontrollers. Students will be required to demonstrate in the exam that they understand the design process and the approach to implementation. The topics and style of questions will be covered and practised in the interactive-style classes throughout the session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Formal exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Demonstrate understanding of design process and the approach to implementation.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To test the student’s knowledge on combinational and sequential logic circuits and PIC microcontrollers</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2 hours and 10 minutes reading time</Data></Cell>
