   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">31285</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mobile Applications Development</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Programming exercises. These are hands-on exercises which are done in the lab and at home. This assessment is designed to test the students on a set of topics presented during the lecture and pre-class study material. Students work together during class to design their solutions to the exercises and share their knowledge, with the implementation to be completed at home and submitted via PLATE prior to the start of the next class. Although collaboration is strongly encouraged, each student must write and submit their own code. You will be peer marked by fellow students, and these marks will be moderated by the instructor. This subject is very practical in nature, and keeping up with the weekly laboratory exercises is essential. All coding in this subject will be carried out using the Java programming language and Android platform.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Programming exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week range</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 2 to Week 7              There are 5 mandatory tasks plus an optional bonus task which is distributed in Week 6. Unless otherwise specified, you will have one week to complete these tasks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">There are 5 mandatory tasks plus an optional bonus task which is distributed in Week 6. Unless otherwise specified, you will have one week to complete these tasks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will apply Android programming techniques to build a set of small Android programs. In undertaking this activity, students will develop their research, problem solving and programming skills to create an effective solution.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is an individual in-class restricted open-book test where you will be tested on Android concepts. You will be permitted to bring two A4 pages of written notes (two single-sided A4 pages or one double-sided A4 page) only into the test.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mid-session test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2 hrs</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class in Week 7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The purpose of this assessment task is for students to apply fundamental knowledge learnt in this subject to the problem solving and design requirements of Android applications development.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is an individual assignment. In this assignment you develop an innovative, medium-sized Android application for a smart phone or Tablet. The application is reported in a design document. Students are required to submit a project proposal by Week 5 and an initial software design document in Week 8. In Weeks 8, 9, 10 and 11, students will work on their projects in the lab and discuss problems and solutions face to face with the instructor. Snapshots of your work must also be uploaded into PLATE at least once per week to demonstrate progress. In Week 12, students submit their source code and final software design document through PLATE and present their working application to the instructor, and answer any questions about the application its source code. Feedback on your application, code and report will be given face to face during this demonstration.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Programming project and report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will develop an innovative, medium-sized application for a smart phone or tablet that can be commercialised. This will involve problem solving and design skills aquired in this subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
