   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41012</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Programming for Mechatronic Systems</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In a Mechatronics System we would use a class to represent a real-world object such as a Sensor. In large projects to facilitate testing it is common to develop a mock (fake) sensor class that behaves the same as the real sensor. This allows for some system testing without the presence of all hardware. Create such a mock sensor class that encapsulates attributes of a specific sensor and a main that interacts with the sensor class. The solution will need to provide appropriate access to sensor attributes and enable access to underlying sensor data. Supply a ZIP file containing the C++ source code of the sensor and a main via the Assignments section on UTSOnline. Further details, including the sensor allocation per student are available on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Developing Sensor Class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">05/04/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Sunday 5 April 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To enhance skills in utilising classes, functions, pointers and macros in developing sensor class on a provided mechatronics sensor specification.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In a Mechatronics System, sensors producing data of similar nature are often abstracted from a base sensor. This allows treating a suite of sensors in an abstract manner when processing the data, the remaining of our processing code can be agnostic to the sensor type. Create a base sensor class and expand it to a range of sensors. Further, create a class to process this data that is agnostic to the sensor type and process that data to produce data that is fused from sensor output. Supply a ZIP file containing the C++ source code and inline source mark-up to enable generating documentation with doxygen via the Assignments section on UTSOnline. Further details, including the sensor allocation per student are available on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Utilising Abstraction for a Range of Sensor Classes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">26/04/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Sunday 26 April 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Skills in utilising, classes, functions, pointers and utilising abstraction, encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism with appropriate documentation will be assessed.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In a Mechatronics System, sensors produce data at varying rates. Decisions need to be made based on correctly associated data in near real-time. Threading and synchronisation are ways to ensure the system performs as intended, with guarantees on the responsiveness of the system to incoming data changes, processing constraints and system behaviour. Create a number of threads that can handle buffering incoming data in an agnostic manner. Further, create a separate thread to process the data from the sensors that ensures synchronisation of the data between producers / consumers and produces an output by extrapolation of data. Supply a ZIP file containing the C++ source code and inline source mark-up to enable generating documentation with doxygen via the Assignments section on UTSOnline. Further details, including the sensor allocation per student are available on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Threading, Synchronisation and Data Integrity</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">24/05/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Sunday 24 May 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Skills in utilising, classes, abstraction, data structures, threading, data synchronisation and documentation will be assessed.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In a Mechatronics System, sensors produce data at varying rates. Decisions need to be made based on correctly associated data in near real-time. Threading and synchronisation are ways to ensure the system performs as intended, with guarantees on the responsiveness of the system to incoming data changes, processing constraints and system behaviour. Functions that exploit the data require unit testing to ensure they behave correctly. Documentation of your own code allows other developers to utilise it as intended and anticipate outcomes, in the same fashion you would use an API. Write a series of components using the ROS CBSE framework that will process data originating from a range of sensors on a simulated robot. Employ appropriate multi-threading and data structures to enable time synchronisation and subsequently interrogation of data which allow simple actions of a robotic platform. Enable appropriate auto-generated documentation by utilising inline source mark-up. Exploit the ROS unit testing framework with test cases evaluating code. Supply a ZIP file containing the source code of the components (with inline source mark-up to enable generating documentation and unit testing) via the Assignments section on UTSOnline. Further details, including the projects available, as well as the Oral Defence (VIVA) particulars, are supplied on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">21/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Sunday 21 June 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To analyse a set of specifications, design, test, document and practically evaluate code to perform analysis of sensor data and simple actions based on data on a simulated robotic platform.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quizzes set out to test the student’s knowledge on Object Oriented Programming with C++, in an incremental manner and provide feedback to students throughout the session. In each quiz students are provided with a programming stub (code base) that is designed to perform a set task. The code base is not functional, students are required to rectify / further develop this code base to accomplish the set task. Submission of student code for assessment is via a code repository, access requires UTS credentials. Following the submission of the quiz, students will be required to complete a peer review of a submission by your classmate. The peer reviews are delegated in a manner to enable you to contrast various approaches to solving the quiz. Failure to submit the peer review will result in 0% being awarded for the quiz.  Submission of peer review marks is via a Google Form, access to form requires UTS credentials. Further information, including guidance on how to obtain the code base, is available on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Review Quizzes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
