   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">31272</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project Management and the Professional</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Using analysis skills and knowledge of ethical perspectives, students will research at least three professional codes of conduct and then analyse stakeholder positions within a supplied case study. This analysis will be in regards to both personal views of the scenario and comparing contents of the ethical codes investigated. The student will provide insight into the scenario from these multiple perspectives and prepare a professionally presented report discussing rationales for the various ethical positions involved.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ethics Assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Approximately 2800 to 3500 words of body (i.e. not counting title page, exec summary, toc, etc.)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">7</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 7              (per assignment outline) Submitted soft copy to UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">(per assignment outline) Submitted soft copy to UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Using a provided scenario student groups will formulate a professional project briefing document suitable for senior management. This report will include business objectives, scoped requirements, project task breakdown and network sequence, project development methodology, budget analysis, charted timelines, quality concerns, risk management, etc. plus additional items noted on the assignment specification. The document should be regarded as if to be submitted to a Board of Directors so professional presentation, formatting and clear communication - as well as accuracy, consistency and relevance of content detail - are important. Video presentation is to be short ‘pitch’ to a senior audience covering the key messages from the report and positively presenting major findings, decisions and summations. A peer assessment (in individually sealed envelope or via email to the tutor) must be included by each student in relation to team members contributing to a report.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project Proposal</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report: 9000 to 10000 words of body (i.e. not counting title page, exec summary, tocs, reference list, etc.) Video: 10 to 12 minutes (MS PowerPoint with narration is suitable)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12              (per assignment outline) Submitted in soft copy to UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">(per assignment outline) Submitted in soft copy to UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tutorial participation involves: relevant written and verbal contribution in class exercises; results from assigned homework; use of UTSOnline discussion boards; the organisation, running and active contribution to weekly workshop/tutorial inputs and outputs; plus assessment of punctually submitted workshop reports. Feedback will be provided verbally in-tutorial regarding the above deliverables and via independent written assessment/review of workshop report submissions (from week 3) and with in-forum responses to student use of UTSOnline (from week 1).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Workshop and Tutorial Activities</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An open book exam held during the formal examination period. This exam is 2 hours long, requires a good level of English comprehension, familiarity with subject content and ability to write well argued answers. Hand written responses must be readable.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
