   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">32933</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Research Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The deliverables in terms of the finished research project will be as follows: Students, working in teams of three or individually, will need to discuss with their supervisor whether a research report alone will be appropriate, or whether a digital product is to be developed. The digital product might consist of a prototype, system, app, website, multimedia product or other digital product to be designed, developed and tested by the student. If this is part of the student's research, then a short report (e.g., 2,000-3,000 words) will also need to be submitted to justify the rationale and need for the prototype/system/product, together with any testing completed. If the student's research plan, as agreed by the supervisor, does not consist of the production of any system, then a more substantial Research Report (approximate length: 8,000-10,000 words) will be the sole deliverable for this assessment task. An alternative to the Research Report is for the student to submit a finished research article to a peer-refereed academic journal or conference. Students are required to come to a written agreement with their supervisor by the end of Week 1 as to what their precise deliverables will be. They should make this in writing, either on a sheet of paper to be signed by all parties or by exchange of emails. Both students and supervisor should keep a copy of the agreement in case of dispute. It should be clear that the intellectual effort expended on Research Project (6cp) is no less than that normally required to pass course work of the same number of credit points. Where students are working in a team, all members of a team will be required to submit a Peer Assessment form with their assignment on the due date. The deliverables will be marked as a whole, but the individual mark will depend on the individual contribution, which will be assessed by the group members. When a conflict occurs, the supervisor’s assessment will overwrite the peer assessment. As a result, the members in the same group may end up having different marks. Assessment criteria: The marking sheet will be published on the subject site. Criteria may vary depending on the nature of the main research deliverables (report, prototype, series of vodcasts, set of multimedia resources, etc.). It is the responsibility of students to pass the marking sheets to their supervisors.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Research Report/Prototype/Multimedia Product</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">80</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 11              The research report / prototype / multimedia product is due by 5 pm, Friday October 16, 2020, delivered to the supervisor in a manner agreed and submit one identical copy to a specific assignment folder in Canvas. For the projects that have prototypes, upload the prototype source materials to a specific assignment folder. When the size of material file is too large to be uploaded, please submit a link to its location where the subject coordinator can download the files.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The research report / prototype / multimedia product is due by 5 pm, Friday October 16, 2020, delivered to the supervisor in a manner agreed and submit one identical copy to a specific assignment folder in Canvas. For the projects that have prototypes, upload the prototype source materials to a specific assignment folder. When the size of material file is too large to be uploaded, please submit a link to its location where the subject coordinator can download the files.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The purpose of this task is to assess a participant's competence in the following aspects:  Demonstrated sound understanding of the current developments in a selected ICT and/or professional practice. Demonstrated good knowledge of research principles and methods applicable to a selected ICT disciplinary area. Skills to reflect critically on theory and professional practice in a selected ICT area. Skills to investigate, analyse and synthesise complex information, problems, concepts and theories or techniques. Skills to apply technical knowledge, IT research methodology, and appropriate tools and resources to meet specified research objectives. Skills to design, implement and evaluate components, systems or processes, and to analyse the results. Ability to apply established theories and/or technology to solve technically challenging ICT problems Ability to use of core body of specialised, in-depth, knowledge for the research topic in a selected ICT disciplinary area. Ability to present the development of his or her project, including background, problem formulation, assumptions, modelling, experiments and results with discussions, in a succinct and cogent form to specialist and non-specialist audience. Professional judgement and/or decision making at a level that would be reasonably expected from a near ICT postgraduate. Demonstrated awareness of legislation, statutory requirements standards and codes of practice relevant to your research project, if applicable. Demonstrated ability to comprehend, analyse and quantify the nature of risk, both of an IT technical kind and in relation to clients, users, the community and the environment and devise strategies for managing this risk, if applicable</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will present their research findings through a video presentation on or before the due date in Week 11. The presentations will be assessed by the project supervisor(s) and the subject coordinator. The presentation mark will be calculated in the following procedure: 1) Calculate the supervisor's mark if more than one supervisor is in the assessment panel. The mark will be the average mark of all suprvisors' marks. 2) Calculate the final mark: The final mark will be the average of marks given by the supervisor and the subject coordinator, respectively. Assessment criteria: The marking criteria will be published on the subject site. Students should pass the marking sheet to the supervsior.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Research Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Practicum period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Practicum period              The face-to-face presentations will start from Week 12. The time and venue of each presentation will be  published in a presentation schedule sheet in Google Drive (you need a gmail account to access this sheet).    Note that students need to register for the face-to-face presentation slots. Since your supervisors need to attend the face-to-face presentation sessions, they need to be consulted and agree on the registered presentation time slots. No show in the scheduled slots will result in zero marks for the assignment 2.   Apart from face-to-face presentations, project groups can deliver their presentations using videos. If taking this option, a group needs to record the presentation as a short video (up to 20 mins), upload it to a host location and submit its link to a specific Google sheet (will be available in Week 11). In the video, all presenters need to show their faces. In the past semester, many project groups took this option and this option becomes favourable due to its flexibility.   Please note that project groups need to submit the presentation materials such as ppt slides to a specific assignment folder. If the presentations are created using online tools, please submit the link to the presentation to the folder. The due date for the submissions is 11PM on Friday 23 October 2020.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The face-to-face presentations will start from Week 12. The time and venue of each presentation will be  published in a presentation schedule sheet in Google Drive (you need a gmail account to access this sheet).    Note that students need to register for the face-to-face presentation slots. Since your supervisors need to attend the face-to-face presentation sessions, they need to be consulted and agree on the registered presentation time slots. No show in the scheduled slots will result in zero marks for the assignment 2.   Apart from face-to-face presentations, project groups can deliver their presentations using videos. If taking this option, a group needs to record the presentation as a short video (up to 20 mins), upload it to a host location and submit its link to a specific Google sheet (will be available in Week 11). In the video, all presenters need to show their faces. In the past semester, many project groups took this option and this option becomes favourable due to its flexibility.   Please note that project groups need to submit the presentation materials such as ppt slides to a specific assignment folder. If the presentations are created using online tools, please submit the link to the presentation to the folder. The due date for the submissions is 11PM on Friday 23 October 2020.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This item is for assessing a participant's oral communication skills and ability to present arguments clearly and concisely to specialist and non-specialist audiences.</Data></Cell>
