   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48370</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Road and Transport Engineering</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to complete an assignment that is related to the content delivered in Weeks 1 to 6. The assignment will be separated into two parts. The first part focuses on students understanding and applying the fundamental components of traffic flow theory. Students will have to solve a series of problems, extending from the problems discussed during lectures and tutorials. The second part of the assignment tests students on their capabilities of developing traffic management solutions for a real world scenario. Students will investigate a traffic management application case study and, using the knowledge gained within the lectures and tutorials, develop solutions for the specific case. Students will need to present the solutions in a report format, similar to what is expected in practice and will be assessed on technical accuracy and clarity in presentation. This task prepares engineering students for transport operations assessment tasks typical of what is required in professional practice.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Transport Modelling and Operations</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">35</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">27/04/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 27 April 2020              Online submission made through UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online submission made through UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment task is designed to evaluate a student’s ability to apply the theoretical concepts of traffic engineering and develop solutions to real-world operational problems within a road network.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to complete an assignment related to transport systems planning with a particular focus on public transport operations. The topics within the report focus on dwell-time management for trains, service scheduling and level of service implications for customer experience. Students will be required to submit a report responding the project brief provided by the lecturer.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Transport Planning</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">35</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">01/06/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11.59pm Monday 1 June 2020              Online submission made through UTSOnline</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online submission made through UTSOnline</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students must attend tutorials to participate in the assessment task and submit individual assignments.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The purpose of this task is to assess a student’s ability to complete a set of divergent thinking activities fundamental to transport planning. Specifically, the task focuses on a student’s ability to define transport systems and relationships, and analyse the impact of operations and behaviour on the performance of these systems.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to undertake a restricted open-book final examination. A formulae sheet will be provided as part of the examination paper. The final examination will cover all material presented during the subject. It will comprise questions from both the Transport Planning and Traffic Operations strands of the subject. The content of the exam will be divided equally (50% split) between each of the 2 strands. Students will be expected to respond to a prescribed case study (provided during the teaching semester) drawing on knowledge from the lectures and tutorials delivered during the semester. The questions will be presented in a format that is 'practical'. Answers will require written responses and calculations. The examination paper embraces the type of questions/problems that are faced daily in the workplace, specific to the transport domain. The questions simulate what the student will face in professional practice, whether on an internship or when employed on a permanent basis.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students are required to demonstrate their ability to undertake necessary calculations and interpret models and designs related to the traffic and transport discipline.</Data></Cell>
