   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48540</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Signals and Systems</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratories form a significant component of the subject. They reinforce the theoretical material and are a lead-up to undertaking the group project. The labs will also provide practical experience in setting up and using specialised test equipment. Students are assessed in a group of two. Each lab requires a significant amount of collective reading, hand-analysis and computer simulation as part of pre-lab work. There is also post-lab work to make sense of the experimental results. Student should complete the prelab work before coming to the lab and get signed by one of the tutor during the lab hours. The pre-lab work, lab work and post-lab work are required to submit as a lab report with cover sheet in the following week for assessment. Each lab carries 4 marks. There are 5 Labs with the following objectives: Lab 1 - Perform various and wide-ranging measurements tasks using a DSO. Lab 2 - Design and test a sampling and reconstruction scheme. Lab 3 - Investigate time-domain and frequency-domain behaviour of a first-order system Lab 4 - Design and test a modulation and demodulation scheme Lab 5 - Investigate time-domain and frequency-domain behaviour of a second-order system</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Labs 1-5</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab 1 : Week 5; Lab 2  : Week 6; Lab 3 : Week 8; Lab 4 : Week 9; Lab 5 : Week 10;</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab 1 : Week 5; Lab 2  : Week 6; Lab 3 : Week 8; Lab 4 : Week 9; Lab 5 : Week 10;</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Skills in modelling and practical applications of signal theory.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is a mid-session closed book quiz to test knowledge of continuous-time signals and systems. It is for 2 Hr and 10 minutes quiz, and consists of 4 long answer type questions with 5 marks each. Students are assessed on the individual basis based on application of theory learned in the class to correctness of approach, methodology and the final answer.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 7, In Class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 7, In Class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test knowledge of continuous-time signals and systems.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is group project with students operating in a group of 2 to analyse a set of specifications, design, simulate, test and practically demonstrate a simple control and communication scheme in the lab. Further, students are required to design and test 3 simple control schemes and design and test an AM coherent demodulator. At the end of the semester, student are required to submit one hand written report per group for final assessment.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 13</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To analyse a set of specifications, design, simulate, test and practically demonstrate a simple control scheme and a simple communication scheme.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 13</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is restricted open book exam for 2 Hr and 10 minutes consisting of 5 questions (4 out of 5 questions with 10 marks each) to test knowledge of discrete-time systems, control systems and communication systems. Students are allowed to bring a copy of the textbook (Lathi, B.P., Linear Systems and Signals, Oxford University Press, 2005. ISBN 0-19-515833-4) and note (48540 Signals and Systems Notes by Dr. Peter McLean). Students are assessed on the individual basis based on the application of theory learned in the class to correctness of approach, methodology and the final answer.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2 hours plus 10 minutes reading time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
