   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48330</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Soil Behaviour</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students can submit the detailed solutions to the Soil Mechanics assignments in online assignment drop box on UTSOnline before the due dates. Nine sets of progressive assignments will be available, covering 7 Chapters in Soil Behaviour. The covering page should include details of the student and the assignment number and title. Feedback will be available online and in the class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Soil Mechanics Assignments (Summative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">As shown on UTSOnline and discussed in the class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">As shown on UTSOnline and discussed in the class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To give students the opportunity to practice problem solving. The assessment task gives students the opportunity to practice various approaches related to soil mechanics and evaluate them. Students will classify, formulate, and solve various problems, and differentiate the different types of solutions.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A list of topics (see UTSOnline) will be provided and students should choose a topic and the side (affirmative (yes) or negative(no)) in the tutorial class. It is recommend to form groups (4 people in one group) with the friends you have in the tutorial class and let the tutor know about members of your group. Each group will be given up to 15min to present their discussion and reasoning in support of their idea. There will be a panel of judges assessing the debates, and in this way formative or feed forward feedback is provided.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Debate Activity (Formative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Presentation</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">As shown on UTSOnline and discussed in the class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">As shown on UTSOnline and discussed in the class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This assessment task provides students with a research inspired learning opportunity to solve real civil/geotechnical engineering challanges and problems. The key purpose is for students to conduct some research on one of the recommended Civil/Geotechnical Engineeering topics and form groups to put their argument “For” or “Against”. The debating format supplements the traditional lecture by engaging students, allowing the lecturer to create an environment that helps students move away from just receiving knowledge into an atmosphere of active participation. Additionally, through debating contemporary issues in the classroom, students can enhance their critical thinking skills.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">There will be two engineering geology practical classes; in the first practical class students will be discussing different rocks and minerals and students can get hand-on experience to identify and discuss many rocks and minerals available in the School of Civil and Environmental Engineering. In the second practical engineering geology stratification and geological map reading and interpretation will be conducted. Students should prepare two written reports and submit on UTSOnline. There are four laboratory sessions covering the topics of soil compaction, soil classification, water flow through soil, consolidation and soil strength. All laboratory sessions will take place in the Soil Mechanics Laboratory. Long trouser and full foot wear are necessary to attend the laboratory classes. Professional laboratory reports should be prepared and submitted for each lab class. Depending on the weather condition and site availability, site visit will be organised to discuss some particular geological features and highlights in NSW particularly Sydney area. This site visit will be supervised by several academics and site condition can be discussed in the field. Students need to provide site visit report.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Practicals and Laboratory Reports (Summative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to the due dates on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To identify sources of uncertainty and errors in soil testing and site investigations, to strengthen statistical analysis and writing skills; to organise teams to conduct laboratory tests, and geological mapping; to providing the students with hands-on experience and to familiarise them with real life problems. In addition, students will be able to write a site visit report (if site visit is organised subjected to the weather condition and site availability).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to the due dates on UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination. This is an open book examination and some questions are related to real-world civil/geotechnical engineering design and contruction.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final Examination (Summative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam Period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam Period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Systematic and critical thinking to obtain ground properties for infrastructure design</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To evaulate the student's understanding of the fundamental concepts, problem solving skills, analysis and design procedures in Engineering Geology and Soil Mechanics and their application in real life. To assess the student's abilities to develop the required objectives of the subject and gain knowledge of Engineering Geology and Soil Mechanics.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A list of topics will be distributed in the tutorial classes and uploaded on UTSOnline, and students need to finalise their topics with the tutor in the tutorial class by Week 7. Topics will be allocated to students in tutorial classes based on first come first serve. Students need to prepare Power Point Slides and Upload on UTSOnline before the due date. Power Point Slides are expected to be original work of the student or if taken from other sources well referenced. Minimum number of slides per presentation is 20 and the file size should be less than 10MB.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Research Project (Summative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Case study</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">15</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Critical review of the literature, finding possible solutions for the problem, and the recommendation for practicing engineers</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To engage with research and to encourage students to be research oriented and life long learners.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Site Visit (Formative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To allow students to demonstrate what they have learnt, to determine what are the difficulties in real practice and on site, and how group discussion and team work can help.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online Quiz (Formative)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
