    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The contributions you make should be carefully considered and written, and you will be assessed over the semester according to the extent to which you:  actively engage with other people’s contributions within the timeframe of the e-tivity. incorporate your personal and professional experience refer to and reflect on set readings, appropriately acknowledging sources in Harvard Style show evidence of and offer ideas from wider reading and research, which may include suggesting additional resources relevant to the topic.  You are required to contribute at least once to every e-tivity beginning after class throughout the semester. Each e-tivity has a set timeframe.  This is an individual assessment which will be marked by the lecturers.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-tivities are designed to complement the tutorial programme, facilitate interaction beyond the classroom and form the foundations for the other assessment tasks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Having formed into your project groups, the purpose of this task is to formalise each member’s role, responsibilities and learning needs for the group project, as well as the general principles by which you will all abide. The charter should:  outline the broad objectives which your group wishes to achieve and how you will achieve them list general rules and principles to which the group has agreed to follow state roles, responsibilities and duties for each group member include a SWOT analysis  The learning contract recognises that each person in your group is likely to have different learning needs according to their prior experience, discipline base and involvement in the multimedia industry. It involves agreement between participants regarding what each person’s learning objectives, strategies, role and responsibilities are to the group. Therefore, the contract is a statement as a group and to each other about what you intend to learn and do for your group project. Therefore, the learning contract should:  detail the learning objectives and strategies for each member, in a way that makes everyone accountable ie strategies should be specific, quantifiable, conditional and relate to the fulfilment of the group’s goals and processes, as well as the completion of the group project refer to the assigned reading Anderson,G., Boud,D. and Sampson,J. (1996) ‘What is a learning contract’ in Learning Contracts: A Practical Guide. London: Kogan Page.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group Charter and learning contract</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Minimum 1000 words per group member.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">4</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 4              Due by 6 pm Workshop in Week 4. Submit via UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due by 6 pm Workshop in Week 4. Submit via UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">You will be assessed according to:   the detail with which you have considered all the work needed to realise the project the detail and accountability of group and individual learning strategies the clarity and feasibility of group and individual objectives balance and consistency across group roles evidence and application of readings on groupwork and Web project management.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">After forming your groups, this exercise allows you to clarify your collective and individual aims, duties, learning needs, objectives and strategies.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Your individual grade for this assessment task will be adjusted following a process of team members rating each other on various performance criteria throughout the semester (Self &amp; Peer Assessment Performance Criteria - Task 5).  The project is where the aims and objectives stated in the group charter and learning contract are implemented. It is to be the outcome of a joint effort from all the members of the group with whom you have negotiated the group charter and learning contract. It involves developing a proposal and plan for producing a multimedia product based on your group’s brief as agreed in Week 4. Your project should:  include all the elements of a human centred design project pre-production phase  show detailed planning of the production process  provide evidence of research, including, where necessary, appropriate references to literature effectively illustrate to your audience how the project will be realised  While you will be assessed on your project as it is submitted.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group project plan and proposal</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Minimum 2000 words per group member.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">12</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 12              Due by 6pm Workshop in Week 12 . Submit via UTSOnline and bring a hard copy to class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">You have been assessed according to:  The inclusion of all the elements of a project pre-production phase; The level of detailed planning of the production process; Evidence of research, including, where necessary, appropriate references to literature; How effectively you illustrate to your client how the project will be realised; The presentation in hard copy format as you would submit it to the client.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task requires groups together to develop a proposal for the production of a multimedia product.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due by 6pm Workshop in Week 12 . Submit via UTSOnline and bring a hard copy to class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The purpose of this task is for you to bring together what you have learned from the groupwork and digital media development process aspect of the subject. You should discuss how successful you felt the group project was with reference to the group charter and learning contract. Your goals for the project should have been a combination of individual and group goals. You should discuss to what extent those goals were achieved. You should make explicit the standards and judgements employed when determining whether this activity was successful ie by what criteria have you judged this project to be successful or otherwise. Describe the kinds of critical incidents that occurred during the group project. You should also choose one critical incident for detailed description. You may find it helpful to create a personal project diary at the beginning of the group project so that you can record dates and times of communications between group members, and keep notes of tasks, comments on decisions and group management issues for inclusion in this section of the report.  Provide a detailed account and analysis of a specific incident related to the operation of your group. This account should include: a description of the event (including the evidence for the occurrence of that event); analysis of the event – what made it happen? What did you like or not like about the event? Are there other interpretations of the event? What does it mean? Is it just? If so, for whom? How did it make you feel? interventions and outcomes – if the incident chosen was one in which the immediate outcome was less than ideal, what interventions were attempted to improve the situation? What were the perceived effects of the intervention strategies? What did you do? knowledge gained – what have you learned from this incident? How will you try to minimise (or maximise) the impact of this type of incident on group effectiveness in the future?</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reflection</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">25</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Minimum 1500 words.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">14</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Week 14              Due by 6 pm Friday 1st November 2019. Submit via UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Due by 6 pm Friday 1st November 2019. Submit via UTSOnline.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The criteria for assessment of this task will be based on the degree to which you demonstrate your learning of the digital media development process in the subject through:  reflecting on the group charter and learning contract, and whether it was useful in informing the way in which your group worked together examining your group’s multimedia development process and how it might have been improved or done differently identifying a critical incident, any underlying assumptions relevant to the behaviour/ reactions to the critical incident, and can explain them; recognising and explaining different perspectives on the issues arising from the critical incident; offering alternative explanations for what you have observed; describing what you have learned from the critical incident and why understanding what could have been improved or done differently in any of the above experiences contributions and discussions on UTSOnline about readings and issues arising from your groupwork acknowledgement of theories and ideas from readings.  Note: that to pass this activity you must demonstrate analysis of the incident beyond mere description of the incident.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The aim of this task is to consolidate what you have learned from the groupwork and multimedia development process aspect of the subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students rate themselves and their group peers anonymously against the performance criteria delveloped and agreed to as part of Group Charter and Learning Contract (Task 2).</Data></Cell>
