   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48642</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Strength of Engineering Materials</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Three in-class problem solving tasks will be conducted in the form of in-class quizzes of 10% each. Quiz 1 will be on the topic of stress analysis and transformation under combined loading, and include determination of internal loading, calculation of normal and shear stresses using stress formulas, demonstration of stress distribution and stress element, and determination of principal stresses by drawing Mohr’s circles and using stress transformation formulas.  Quiz 2 will be on the topic of shaft design, and include sketch of bending moment and shear moment diagrams, determination of critical sections, and application of failure theories to calculate the diameter of the shaft.  Quiz 3 will be on the topic of fatigue analysis, and include determine of the estimated fatigue strength, use of S-N equation to calculate the number of cycles to failure, and application of fatigue design formula to determine the factor of safety.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Problem solving tasks</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1) In-class Problem Solving Task Quiz 1, in Lec1, 8:00-9:00, Monday, 13/April/2020.   2) In-class Problem Solving Task Quiz 2, in Lec1, 8:00-9:00, Monday, 4/May/2020.   3) In-class Problem Solving Task Quiz 3, in Lec1, 8:00-9:00, Monday, 1/June/2020.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1) In-class Problem Solving Task Quiz 1, in Lec1, 8:00-9:00, Monday, 13/April/2020.   2) In-class Problem Solving Task Quiz 2, in Lec1, 8:00-9:00, Monday, 4/May/2020.   3) In-class Problem Solving Task Quiz 3, in Lec1, 8:00-9:00, Monday, 1/June/2020.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Problem solving tasks that aim to evaluate the students’ ability to perform stress analysis, failure analysis, and to solve impact loading and statically indeterminate problems. The other associated aims are to develop students’ analytical skills in stress analysis and their knowledge of material properties and failure modes in mechanical design.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This consists of three tasks:  Open &amp; Closed ended PV (7%) requires students to perform the test and take the measurements of strains, then calculate the principal stresses, and submission of a lab report. PV with torsion (8%) focuses on stress and strain measurements under combined loading, calculation of principal strains and stresses at a certain point, and submission of a lab report. Fatigue and Creep (5%) includes the measurement of the cycles to failure under different levels of external loading, fatigue analysis to validate (or compare with) the experimental results, and submission of a lab report.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab work &amp; reports</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1) Open &amp; Closed Ended PV Lab Report due anytime before Week 9, 23:59, Saturday,  16/May/2020. 2) PV with Torsion Lab Report due anytime before Week 9, 23:59, Saturday, 16/May/2020. Online submission. 3) Fatigue Lab Report due anytime before Week 12, 23:59, Saturday,  6/June/2020. (not in Final StuVac)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">1) Open &amp; Closed Ended PV Lab Report due anytime before Week 9, 23:59, Saturday,  16/May/2020. 2) PV with Torsion Lab Report due anytime before Week 9, 23:59, Saturday, 16/May/2020. Online submission. 3) Fatigue Lab Report due anytime before Week 12, 23:59, Saturday,  6/June/2020. (not in Final StuVac)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab work and reports are to develop students’ ability to interpret and analyse measured data and given information related to mechanical properties of materials and communicate effectively. By writing lab reports, students will demonstrate and apply theoretical and practical knowledge of engineering materials to different contexts, and also understand how to perform experimental stress analysis.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final exam usually consists of four questions covering the main contents of the subject. Question 1 will be focussed on stress under combined loading and stress transformation. Question 2 will be on shaft design. Question 3 will be on deflection and statically indeterminate problems. Question 4 will be on strain energy and impact loading.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final exam</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">2 hours plus 10 minutes reading time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Formal Exam during Final Assessment Weeks.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The main aim of the final exam is to provide students with a comprehensive review of stress analysis, failure and fatigue analysis, statically indeterminate problems, strain energy and impact loading. The other aim is to develop students’ analytical skills in strength of engineering materials for machine element design.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Formal Exam during Final Assessment Weeks.</Data></Cell>
