   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">48349</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Structural Analysis</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">After the due date, a copy of each online quiz will be made available for unlimited practice until the end of the teaching session.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Weekly online quizzes (best 8 of 9 quizzes)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">8</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">One quiz per week to be completed before the lecture unless specified otherwise. The best 8 of 9 quizzes will be considered. After the submission deadline no make-up quiz will be offered. Check UTSOnline for more information.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">One quiz per week to be completed before the lecture unless specified otherwise. The best 8 of 9 quizzes will be considered. After the submission deadline no make-up quiz will be offered. Check UTSOnline for more information.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online quizzes will be marked automatically. Some questions will be formula based whereby each student will be given a unique data set and therefore correct numerical answers will differ for each student. Only correct numerical answers entered within a prescribed error tolerance will receive marks. Online quizzes are typically due 5 minutes before the start of the lecture each week. You may attempt the quiz multiple times until the due date and time.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will be required to solve weekly online quizzes that are based on material covered in the lectures, tutorials and reading prescribed prior to attending the lecture. The quizzes are designed to provide students with timely feedback on their learning progress and to encourage them to study regularly before and after the lectures.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Download the eTask from the UTSOnline subject website. Complete the task and upload to UTSOnline by the due date. Each eTask is typically a spread sheet containing 2 structural analysis problems to be solved. In case of multiple submissions, only the last submission will be counted.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">eTasks</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">24</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Approximately one eTask every 2-3 weeks  (See Program). Students will have approximately 2 weeks to complete each eTask and are expected to plan and manage their time in order to submit by the due date.  Late submissions and submissions other than through UTSOnline will not be accepted.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Approximately one eTask every 2-3 weeks  (See Program). Students will have approximately 2 weeks to complete each eTask and are expected to plan and manage their time in order to submit by the due date.  Late submissions and submissions other than through UTSOnline will not be accepted.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The eTasks will be marked using a programmed algorithm, therefore only correct numerical answers entered within a prescribed error tolerance will attract marks. Each student will be given a unique data set, which is based on her/his student ID and therefore correct answers will differ for each student.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Students will be required to complete 3-4 separate eTasks. These are spread sheet based assignments that are downloaded and must be submitted through UTSOnline by the due date. Each student is assigned an eTask with a different data set depending on the student number. The eTasks are challenging assessments that are summative but also intended to be formative. Students will have approximately 2 weeks to complete each eTask. They have the opportunity to collaboratively discuss solution concepts with fellow students including during tutorial sessions to validate their assumptions and solution methodology. Each eTask will contain approximately 2 exercise problems that are based on material covered in the lectures, tutorial sessions and prescribed reading.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Supervised tests on topics covered during the weeks prior to the test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tests (best 3 of 4 tests)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercises</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">18</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Actual time may vary but on average test duration will be typically 30 - 45 minutes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">n/a</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In class in       during Tutorial sessions according to weekly scheduleSee also Further information.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The best 3 of 4 tests will be counted and there will be no makeup or supplementary tests given.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tests are conducted during tutorial classes and aim to simulate exam conditions. Each test will focus on topics covered in lectures and tutorials during the weeks prior to the test. The test will assess the competency of students in selecting and applying methods of structural analysis. Equally important is the ability of the student to present the results in clear form showing solution steps with relevant sketches for free-body diagrams and internal action diagrams.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">during Tutorial sessions according to weekly schedule</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The final exam is open book, and relevant formula sheets will be provided as part of the question paper.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Final examination (centrally conducted)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Examination</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Two hours plus 10 minutes reading time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UTS Exam period              Refer to the UTS timetable of centrally conducted exams.  If you are sick or unwell at the time of the final exam, do not attempt the exam but apply for an alternative exam within two working days after the missed exam. Please follow the instructions at the following website:  https://www.uts.edu.au/current-students/managing-your-course/classes-and-assessment/exams/alternative-examinations Please note that supplementary exams are not routinely offered in this subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Refer to the UTS timetable of centrally conducted exams.  If you are sick or unwell at the time of the final exam, do not attempt the exam but apply for an alternative exam within two working days after the missed exam. Please follow the instructions at the following website:  https://www.uts.edu.au/current-students/managing-your-course/classes-and-assessment/exams/alternative-examinations Please note that supplementary exams are not routinely offered in this subject.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This is a comprehensive authentic assessment that covers the entire subject content and will examine the competency of students in selecting and applying appropriate methods of structural analysis, planning the solution approach and clearly and logically presenting it. Equally important are presenting relevant equations, the accuracy of computations and drawing of sketches of free-body diagrams and force and moment diagrams with correct sign conventions. The ability to use diagrammatic reasoning and consider equilibrium and compatibility conditions to qualitatively analyse a structure and correctly predict support reactions, deformed shape and force and moment diagrams will also be examined.</Data></Cell>
