   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">41027</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The Enterprising Professional</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A Career Design Portfolio depicts a curated individual learning journey or roadmap within the subject. Each student will develop a Career Design Portfolio. The Career Design Portfolio may be an electronic portfolio or ePortfolio in the format of student's own choice. Students can build on an existing portfolio if they have one or develop a new one. Students can use the UTSOnline ePortfolio tool or develop their own using other platforms (e.g. Weebly, WordPress). The Career Design Portfolio should begin with an introduction to yourself (course of study, major, work experience, and general career aspirations) a photo and a description of what motivated you to take the subject and what you hope to get out of it (e.g. your objectives). In the first week, the student will flesh out their design portfolio to include the results of their Fingerprint for Success assessment of their enterprising and innovation mindsets and what the student intends to achieve through the subject (their learning contract), including learning goals and tasks to be completed. Students can choose from a variety of learning goals and tasks that are provided or students can supply their own goals and tasks. The Career Design Portfolio should end with a final reflection that synthesizes the student’s development and achievement of the subject learning outcomes throughout the subject journey. The Career Design Portfolio will be assessed at least twice during the session: Assessment 1 Weight 20% (Submission 1)(Sprint 1) Assessment 2 Weight 40% (Submission 2)(Sprint 4)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Career Design Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">60</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submission 1: 11:59pm XXXday XX Month Year, and presented in class on XXXday, XX Month Year. Submission 2: 11:59pm XXXday XX Month Year, and presented in class on XXXday, XX Month Year.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Submission 1: 11:59pm XXXday XX Month Year, and presented in class on XXXday, XX Month Year. Submission 2: 11:59pm XXXday XX Month Year, and presented in class on XXXday, XX Month Year.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Performance Standard to Pass The Career Design Portfolio must include sufficient evidence to demonstrate development and (final) achievement of the SLOs. The Career Design Portfolio must be a professional presentation, with no or few errors (e.g. spelling, grammar, formatting, accessibility). It must be complete and comprehensive. Submission 1 must contain:  An introduction with photo and statement of motivation to take the studio subject and objectives in taking the subject. Your learning contract: what do you intend to achieve through the project, including learning goals and tasks to be completed. What will you learn in that process? What will you produce? You may revise this in subsequent submissions.  Submissions 1 and 2 of the Career Design Portfolio must contain curated artefacts (at least 2 per submission) which illustrates your engagement and development at each sprint and across the subject's six Subject Learning Objectives.  Reflective statements which clearly articulate and demonstrate engagement with all subject teaching and learning activities (including data collected and/or incorporation of expert, facilitator, and peer feedback, as well as, information that emerges from systematic group progress review each week).  By Submission 2 the Career Design Portfolio must contain:  A conclusion articulating how your learning journey has enabled you to meet or redefine your objectives. A final reflective statement that synthesizes your learning journey. Each subject learning objective is addressed in the final reflective statement indicating the ways in which the learning was achieved. Reflectively curated, relevant artefacts that form a coherent and cumulative substantiation of claims of achievement of the subject's Subject Learning Objectives. Presentation of the Career Design Portfolio in class.  Performance Statement/Criteria - Not yet pass (Fail)  Introduction is not included, incomplete or lacks coherence. Career Design Portfolio does not address all of the Subject Learning Objectives. Artefacts are missing. Some artefacts are not included, incomplete or irrelevant. A collection of artefacts is presented but lacks coherence or a compelling illustration of engagement across the Subject Learning Objectives. Reflective statements are either not included, incomplete, lack coherence or are not substantiated by artefacts. Reflective statements indicate little or no engagement with formative and/or other feedback provided. Peer review is not demonstrated at the end of each sprint. Self-evaluation is not demonstrated at the end of each sprint. Submissions of the Career Design Portfolio do not contain:  Evidence of development of the design process and how learning has evolved at each stage. Presentation in class.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For students to demonstrate their achievement of each of the Subject Learning Objectives using a collection of artefacts together with reflections that meet the pass performance criteria.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A Project Design Portfolio depicts a curated team learning journey or roadmap within the subject. Each team will develop a Project Design Portfolio. The Design Portfolio may be an electronic portfolio or ePortfolio in the format of team's own choice. Students can use the UTSOnline ePortfolio tool or develop their own using other platforms (e.g. Weebly, WordPress). The Project Design Portfolio should begin with an introduction to project, the problem being solved, the significance of the problem, and intended target audience. The portfolio should describe the type of innovation, key aspects of the human-centered design, description of the evolution of prototypes and validation processes/data and a reflection on the group learning (challenges and insights about the innovation process). The Project Design Portfolio should end with a final reflection that synthesizes the team’s development and achievement of the subject learning outcomes throughout the subject journey. The Project Design Portfolio will be assessed at least once during the session (at the end).</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Team Project Design Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Portfolio</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">40</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59pm XXXday Month Year, and presented in class on XXXday Month Year.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11:59pm XXXday Month Year, and presented in class on XXXday Month Year.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group and individually assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For students to demonstrate their achievement of each of the Subject Learning Objectives using a collection of artefacts together with reflections that meet the pass performance criteria.</Data></Cell>
