   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">49205</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Transmission Systems</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A short, independent quiz will be held in class during the weeks indicated in the subject program. This will be an online quiz using UTSOnline. A Group quiz will follow this offering peer review, collaboration and discussion.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quizzes</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz/test</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">20</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz 1 : InClass, Week 2; Quiz 2 : InClass, Week 4 Quiz 3 : InClass, Week 6 Quiz 4 : InClass, Week 9 Quiz 5 : InClass, Week 11; Quiz 6 :</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quiz 1 : InClass, Week 2; Quiz 2 : InClass, Week 4 Quiz 3 : InClass, Week 6 Quiz 4 : InClass, Week 9 Quiz 5 : InClass, Week 11; Quiz 6 :</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Individual</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give students regular feedback on important concepts from classes and teach them how to assess their progress against others. The Group quizzes teach the value of interaction and communication. They offer some relative peer assessment of both progress and the level of engagement with online lectures.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Overview: Students work in allocated groups of 4 or 5 to create a journal paper review on an assigned topic. Divide the paper into an abstract, introduction, topic outline, summary, conclusions, and references – see the example IEEE paper provided as a guide on how to put the paper together. In addition please include a brief statement of contribution at the end of the paper outlining what each individual has done – all members must be uniformly in agreement with the claims. Individuals from the group will work on different parts of the paper and must all contribute – author lists generally follow the level of contribution of each author with the strongest contributors listed first. Tasks:  Identify which Group you are in. Identify which Topic is assigned to your group. Working as a Group create a 2-3 page (max) journal paper on UTSOnline on an assigned Topic. Once you have your topic, the group will need to do some research in the UTS Library and/or on the Internet. The paper should aim to address the questions given below. The paper template is provided online and follows the standard IEEE journal submission template. Please follow surname alphabetical order for author listing. Using the Template divide appropriate amount of work amongst each other and ensure everyone contributes fairly. The Group will be required to assess the work of 2 other assigned groups. The assessment of their work needs to be undertaken according to the assessment criteria given below.  Questions to be addressed in the paper:  What is the title of your transmission/internet system topic? Introduce the topic and challenges noting that you will be assessed by peers so clear, but brief and concise, explanations are essential. The paper must be well written and thoughtful. The abstract must be a succinct relevant summary of the paper content. Is the topic covering a standard or standards? If so, what type of standard? Who has issued the standard? Is it publicly available? If so, you should provide a URL to the actual standard document. What year was it released? Are there multiple versions? If so, you should provide a table with details. What type of channel is it to be used with? What is the maximum recommended distance over which the channel can operate without some form of signal conditioning? What type of modulation is used in the system? Describe this briefly and provide a constellation diagram (if appropriate). What is the analogue bandwidth required by a user of this system? If there is more than one mode, you should provide a table with details. What is the data rate of the system? If there is more than one mode you should provide a table with details. What is the maximum power or energy that is used by your system? Can you characterize your system by the amount of energy per bit? You may need to make some assumptions to calculate this. State your assumptions and show your working. Importantly, you must identify what is the dominant form of noise in your system and how do you know this? Plot your system’s operating point on the bandwidth efficiency plane. If your system operates within a certain range, plot the best and worst case conditions. You should show all working clearly identifying how you arrived at your data points. What are the limits and future challenges of the system or systems? Ensure you have at least two references from the last year in your reference list.  Assessment Criteria:  Have questions (1 to 11) been addressed satisfactorily? Is the paper clear in explaining a technology or technology feature and is it insightful – does it offer more than what is asked and does it offer something original? Are contributors fairly identified? (40%) Is the information provided relevant and up-to-date, accurate and concise? Have calculations and results been verified? Have references to scholarly information sources been provided in UTS Harvard format? Are acronyms defined? (30%) Does the paper follow the format required? Is it well written, clearly articulated and succinct? Are the references suitable and current? Is the information provided in a clear, logical format that is well structured, pleasing to the eye, free of grammatical and spelling errors? (30%)  Peer Assessment: Your group must submit a 1-page assessment report on each of the assigned papers to review. The author list must be in reverse format to that for your submitted paper. The reports must provide an evidence-based assessment of the papers based on the specified assessment criteria. “Evidence based” means that you have to actually refer to examples in the paper to demonstrate that a particular criterion has been met. If you have a contradiction, evidence must be used to support this including citing contradictory literature. All references must be checked as part of this assessment. A mark out of 10 (produced using the previously mentioned criteria) must be provided at the bottom of the page for each paper assessed. Resources: How can I find information? http://lib.uts.edu.au and http://www.google.com.au. IEEE standards are available to UTS students through IEEE Xplore which can be accessed via the library home page. What is a “scholarly information source? http://ctl.yale.edu/writing/using-sources/scholarly-vs-popular-sources What is UTS Harvard referencing format? http://www.lib.uts.edu.au/help/referencing Submission Requirements: Peer assessments: must be uploaded via the &gt;&gt;View/Complete link in UTSOnline in the ASSESSMENT TASKS section</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assignment - Research Paper</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">30</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Custom</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project Assignment Research Paper Due at 5:59 PM, 15 May 2020 (20%) Research Paper Peer Assignment Due at 11:59 PM, 30 May 2020 (10%)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Project Assignment Research Paper Due at 5:59 PM, 15 May 2020 (20%) Research Paper Peer Assignment Due at 11:59 PM, 30 May 2020 (10%)</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To allow students to develop a deeper understanding of some aspects of the subject material and to demonstrate a capacity to undertake literature searches as would be expected in a real workplace and research environment. Telecommunication and sensing engineers typically perform and review research on existing and new technologies and compare these in terms of the fundamental tradeoffs to existing technologies when designing new systems or upgrading existing systems either for telecommunications or for the internet-of-things.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">This task involves finding a piece of technology available commercially online on the internet and assessing its specifications and details according to the required list to be provided at the time the exercise is undertaken. This is a group exercise and the choice of technology will depend on which group you are in. The group will divide the work and submit a combined, concise summary in a single document.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lab Report</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Laboratory/practical</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Date/Time</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">04/05/2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">05:59 PM</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5.59pm Monday 4 May 2020</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Group, group assessed</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">To understand some aspects of the subject more deeply and gain some practical hands-on skills.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The final exam will be a 2 hour online exam, similar in style and level of difficulty as the quiz questions. This is a &quot;restricted open book&quot; exam and you will be permitted to have one double sided A4-sized page of handwritten, original notes in the exam.</Data></Cell>
