   <Row>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">32133</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">e-Market Trading Technology</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Design, construction and analysis of a software trading agent (implemented in Java) that will compete with other students' trading agents in an e-Market designed in the Faculty. The trading agent assignment is submitted in two parts:  Part A submitted at 20:00 on 4 April 2013 (before Tournament Day that is 8 April 2013). Your trading agent design, rationale, strategies, code and performance — Part A counts for 80% of the Trading Agent Assignment mark. Part B submitted on 13 April 2013 (after Tournament Day that is 8 April 2013). A report of your reflections on how you could have improved your agent — Part B counts for 20% of the Trading Agent Assignment mark.  You should submit Part A by 20:00 on 4 April 2013. Your report together with your code and basic documentation should be submitted electronically in a .zip file as an email attachment to:  john.debenham@uts.edu.au  and you should receive an email receipt within 24 hours. If you do not receive a receipt then contact the subject co-ordinator. Part B of the trading agent assignment consists of:  a two page report of your reflections on how you could have improved your agent, particularly in the light of your experience on Tournament Day 8 April 2013. You should refer back to your report in Part A, should describe how your four strategies could have been improved, and should describe the rationale for those improvements. Note that in this competitive environment there is no &quot;best&quot; solution. It is always possible to make a better agent. [20 marks]  You should submit Part B by 18:00 on 13 April 2013. Your report should be submitted electronically in a .zip file as an email attachment to:  john.debenham@uts.edu.au  and you should receive an email receipt within 24 hours. If you do not receive a receipt then contact the subject co-ordinator.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Trading Agent assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In assessing written reports marks will not be given on the basis of the volume of text submitted. Be brief. The art of scientific writing is to say what you want to say succinctly. As a guide, for your report in Part A, ten good pages will get more marks than forty pages of waffle.  As a guide, the Trading Agent Assignment is expected to take 30 hours work in addition to timetabled classes.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Not applicable; see Further information</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Understand problem context Design system Analyse system performance Refine solution and reflect on observations</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">There will be one Market Assignment that counts for 50% of the total mark. The Market Assignment will consist of two parts:  Part 1 the written report comprising:  the completed Islander tutorial — it is on the disc that you have been given [30 marks] the design of a performative structure for your (small, say 3-5 scenes) market system — you are expected to identify a problem that could be solved using the Electronic Institutions metaphor and specify this institution [30 marks] [if you are looking for a very high mark] a working validated system [30 marks]   Part 2 the presentation of your work on “Demonstration Day” 6 June 2013 [10 marks]  Your assignment (as a doc file or a pdf) should be “zipped” and sent to Dr Anton Bogdanovych: anton@it.uts.edu.au by email by 18:00 on 6 June 2013. You should receive an email reply with a brief receipt that you should keep as proof of submission. Assignments are normally marked within two weeks of submission and may be collected from the lecturer by arrangement.</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Market assignment</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">50</Data></Cell>
    <Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In assessing written reports marks will not be given on the basis of the volume of text submitted. Be brief. The art of scientific writing is to say what you want to say succinctly. As a guide, for your report, ten good pages will get more marks than forty pages of waffle.  As a guide, the Market Assignment is expected to take 30 hours work in addition to timetabled classes.</Data></Cell>
